17592186314902 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186119961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314903 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186119963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314853 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186119963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314855 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186119963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314857 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186119963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314858 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117819 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314140 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120285 1071 "Consider the curve $r(t) = ⟨2t, 1 - 3t, 5 + 6t⟩$, $t \\ge 0$, for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s = 7t$. Find $R(s)$, the parametrization of the curve by its arc length $s$." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120285 1071 "Consider the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,1-3t,\\,5+6t⟩$, $t\\ge 0$, for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=7t.$ Find $R(s),$ the parametrization of the curve by its arc length $s.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120285 1074 "First, since $s=7t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{7}.$\n\nThe vector function that pametrizes the curve $r(t)$ by its arc length $s$, with $s\\ge 0$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{7}) \\\\  & =⟨2(\\frac{s}{7}),\\,1-3(\\frac{s}{7}),\\,5+6(\\frac{s}{7})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}⟨2s,\\,7-3s,\\,35+6s⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117831 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314532 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120287 1071 "Which integral  expression gives the arc length of the curve $f(t)={e}^{t}(\\,i+2\\,j+t\\,k)$ from  $t=1$ to $t=2?$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120287 1071 "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $f(t) = e^{t}(i + 2j + tk)$ from $t = 1$ to $t = 2$?" 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120287 1074 "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{t}\\,j+(t{e}^{t}+{e}^{t})\\,k \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{({e}^{t})^{2}+(2{e}^{t})^{2}+({e}^{t}(t+1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}({t}^{2}+2t+1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}(1+4+{t}^{2}+2t+1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+6}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117843 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314410 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120289 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1, 0, 3)$ and is parallel to the vectors $v_{1} = ⟨5, 2, 1⟩$ and $v_{2} = ⟨0, 3, 3⟩$." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120289 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,0,3)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨5,2,1⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,3,3⟩.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120289 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,0,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =3i-15j+15k \\\\  & =⟨3,-15,15⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =⟨1,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =48 \\\\ 3x-15y+15z & =48 \\\\ 3x-15y+15z-48 & =0 \\\\ x-5y+5z-16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117879 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314297 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120295 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:{\\begin{aligned}x=4+3t \\\\ y=3+4t \\\\ z=-2+6t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:2x+5y+8z=229.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120295 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n$l: {x = 4 + 3t; y = 3 + 4t; z =-2 + 6tandΠ: 2x + 5y + 8z = 229$." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120295 1074 "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y+8z & =229 \\\\ 2(4+3t)+5(3+4t)+8(-2+6t) & =229 \\\\ 8+6t+15+20t-16+48t & =229 \\\\ 74t & =222 \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=3$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4+3(3)=13 \\\\ y=3+4(3)=15 \\\\ z=-2+6(3)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $P(13,15,16).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117885 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314057 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120296 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:{\\begin{aligned}x=5-2t \\\\ y=-1+4t \\\\ z=-3-5t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:2x-5y+3z-84=0.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120296 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n$l: {x = 5 - 2t; y =-1 + 4t; z =-3 - 5tandΠ: 2x - 5y + 3z - 84 = 0$." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120296 1074 "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5y+3z-84 & =0 \\\\ 2(5-2t)-5(-1+4t)+3(-3-5t) & =84 \\\\ 10-4t+5-20t-9-15t & =84 \\\\ -39t & =78 \\\\ t & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=-2$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=5-2(-2)=9 \\\\ y=-1+4(-2)=-9 \\\\ z=-3-5(-2)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is  $P(9,-9,7).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117897 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314546 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120298 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-1, 2,-3)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r - ⟨1,-5, 3⟩) \\cdot ⟨1, 0, 4⟩ = 0$." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120298 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-1,2,-3)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-⟨1,-5,3⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,0,4⟩=0.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120298 1074 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(1,-5,3).$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,-5,3⟩-⟨-1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-7,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=⟨1,0,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2\\cdot 1+(-7)\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 4|}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2+0+24|}{\\sqrt{1+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|26|}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{26\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117909 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314490 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120300 1071 "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $Span{a_{1}, a_{2}}$, where\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120300 1071 "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120300 1074 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-7 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 1 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & 1 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k-28\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k-28\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-3.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-3\\,⇒\\,k=3.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117915 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120301 1071 "Given that $Q(4, 0,-3)$ lies on the plane $Π: r \\cdot ⟨1,-4, 5⟩ =-11$, find the shortest distance from $P(-2, 1,-1)$ to $Π$." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120301 1071 "Given that $Q(4,0,-3)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,5⟩=-11$, find the shortest distance from $P(-2,1,-1)$ to $Π.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120301 1074 "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨4,0,-3⟩-⟨-2,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,-1,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector $n=⟨1,-4,5⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-4)+(-2)\\cdot 5|}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{5}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6+4-10|}{\\sqrt{1+16+25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,0\\,|}{\\sqrt{42}} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $P$ lies on the plane $Π.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117921 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314084 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120302 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(2, 1, 3)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $3x - 6y - 6z =-15$." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120302 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(2,1,3)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $3x-6y-6z=-15.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120302 1074 "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=1$ and $y=-1$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-6y-6z & =-15 \\\\ 3(1)-6(-1)-6z & =-15 \\\\ -6z & =-24 \\\\ z & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(1,-1,4)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,-1,4⟩-⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨3,-6,-6⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-6)+1\\cdot (-6)|}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-6)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-3+12-6|}{\\sqrt{9+36+36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117927 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314091 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120303 1071 "Given that $Q(1, 3, 5)$ lies on the plane $Π: r \\cdot ⟨ - 4, 1,-3⟩ =-16$, find the shortest distance from $P(-3, 7, 3)$ to $Π$." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186120303 1071 "Given that $Q(1,3,5)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨-4,1,-3⟩=-16$, find the shortest distance from $P(-3,7,3)$ to $Π.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186120303 1074 "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,3,5⟩-⟨-3,7,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-4,1,-3⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|4\\cdot (-4)+(-4)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-3)|}{\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-16-4-6|}{\\sqrt{16+1+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-26|}{\\sqrt{26}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186124667 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314788 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186124667 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314789 131941395