"Consider the boundary value problem\n$y^{″} + 4λ^{2}y = 0, y(0) = y(1) = 0, 0 \\le x \\le 1, λ \\ge 0$.\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem.\nThe eigenvalues are $λ_{n} =$ {{field-1}}, where $n ∈ N$.\n\nThe eigenfunctions are $y_{n}(x) =$ {{field-2}}, where $B_{n} ∈ R$." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167272 1074 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+4{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 2\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\lambda )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n2\\lambda =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164815 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164820 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314550 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314691 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167275 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8\\lambda {y}^{′}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167275 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y^{″} - 8λy^{′} + 16λ^{2}y = 0, y^{′}(1) = 0, y(2) + 2y^{′}(2) = 0, 1 \\le x \\le 2, λ ∈ R$.\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\nThe eigenvalues are $λ =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe eigenfunctions are $y =$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167275 1074 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-8\\lambda m+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-4\\lambda )(m-4\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=4\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{4\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda x}+B{e}^{4\\lambda x}+4B\\lambda x{e}^{4\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4A\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda }+B{e}^{4\\lambda }+4B\\lambda {e}^{4\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 4A\\lambda +B+4B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+2B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(4A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(4A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+8A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+(4+16\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+8\\lambda )A+4(1+4\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda  & 1+4\\lambda  \\\\ 1+8\\lambda  & 4(1+4\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+4\\lambda )-(1+4\\lambda )(1+8\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -(1+8\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )[16\\lambda -1-8\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+4\\lambda )(8\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{8},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{4{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(4\\lambda )A+(1+4\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164876 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164879 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314046 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314539 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167281 1071 "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167281 1071 "$⟨f(x), g(x)⟩ = \\displaystyle \\int_{- π}^{π}f(x)g(x)dx$\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x ∈ [- π, π)$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n$⟨\\sin (3x), \\sin (3x)⟩ =$\n{{field-1}}\n\nThe functions $f(x) = \\sin (3x)$ and $g(x) = \\sin (3x)$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167281 1074 "We begin by using the half-angle identity\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ $\\text{are not orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164892 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314082 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167284 1071 "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167284 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\sin (5x)\\sin (x)dx$?" 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167284 1074 "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x-x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164925 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164930 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314213 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314565 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167289 1071 "Let $F(x) =-5x$ for $x ∈ [0, π)$. Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2π$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.$(2(-1)^{n})/(n)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167289 1071 "Let $F(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167289 1074 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{10}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164941 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314268 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167291 1071 "Let $F(x) = x^{2} - 2x$ for $x ∈ [0, π)$, and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x ∈ (- π, π)$. What is $f(x)$ for $x ∈ (- π, 0)$?" 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167291 1071 "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167291 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{2}-2(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164964 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314034 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167295 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}+2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167295 1071 "$f(x)∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (nx)$\nLet $F(x) = 3x^{2} + 2$ for $x ∈ [0, π)$. The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2π$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for $a_{0}$.$a_{0} =$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167295 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(3{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[{x}^{3}+2x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }({\\pi }^{3}+2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2({\\pi }^{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164965 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314642 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167296 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186167296 1071 "$f(x)∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (nx)$\nLet $F(x) =-\\sin (\\frac{x}{3})$ for $x ∈ [0, π)$. The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2π$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for $a_{0}$.$a_{0} =$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186167296 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{3}{2}-3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176089 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314977 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176089 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314980 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176089 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314981 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176089 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314990 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315004 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315005 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315032 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315033 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314762 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314763 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314764 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314765 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314766 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314767 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314768 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174970 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186176477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314126 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176477 1071 "The rate at which the population of a particular country grows $P(t)$, after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dP}{dt} = 0.02P$.\nHow many years does it take for the population to double? Round your answer to the nearest year.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186176477 1071 "The rate at which the population of a particular country grows $P(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.02P.\n$$\n\nHow many years does it take for the population to double? Round your answer to the nearest year." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176477 1074 "We model the population growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.02P.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.02t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial population.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the population of the country is double the initial number. That is,\n\n$$\nP({t}_{1})=2{P}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & ={P}_{0}{e}^{0.02\\cdot {t}_{1}} \\\\ 2{P}_{0} & ={P}_{0}{e}^{0.02{t}_{1}} \\\\ 2 & ={e}^{0.02{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.02{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & =0.02{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.02} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈34.657\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $35$ years for the population to double." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174982 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186176479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314623 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176479 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176479 1071 "The rate at which the number of particles in a radioactive substance decays $N(t)$, after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dN}{dt} =-0.01N$.\n\nHow many years will it take for the number of particles to be $1%$ of the original number? Round your answer to the nearest year.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186176479 1071 "The rate at which the number of particles in a radioactive substance decays $N(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-0.01N.\n$$\n\nHow many years will it take for the number of particles to be $1%$ of the original number? Round your answer to the nearest year." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186176479 1074 "We model the number of particles using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-0.01N.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.01t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial number of particles.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of particles is $1%$ of its initial number. That is,\n\n$$\nN({t}_{1})=0.01{N}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.01{N}_{0} & ={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.01 & ={e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.01{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01) & =-0.01{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.01)}{0.01} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈460.517\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $461$ years for the number of particles to be $1%$ of the original number." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186184244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314760 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186184245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314777 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186184245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314782 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186184246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314792 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186184246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314793 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186184246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314794 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186217256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314748 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186221432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315038 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186221432 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315051 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186221433 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315071 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186221433 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315072 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186221444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314848 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186221444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314849 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186221444 1038 #e