e solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1+1=2.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =1+3\\cdot (-1)+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈0.00.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220740 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186222016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314685 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222016 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222016 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1),\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222016 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = y(2\\ln (x) + 1), y(1) = 1$\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(2)$, rounded to two decimal places.$6.24$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186222016 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+{y}_{\\text{new}}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})+1))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})+1) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})+1) & =\\frac{1}{2}y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})) & =\\frac{1}{2}y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1+1=2.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+3)}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\  & ≈-7.77.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-7.77.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220745 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186222017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314475 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222017 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y({x}^{2}+1),\\,y(-1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(-\\frac{1}{2})$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222017 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = y(x^{2} + 1), y(-1) = 1$\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(-\\frac{1}{2})$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$y(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\approx$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186222017 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) & =y \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y}{1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y}{1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{1-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ≈2.67,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈2.67.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220750 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186222018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220754 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186222018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314116 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314688 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222018 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222018 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-3y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222018 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x - 3y, y(0) = 1$\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$y(\\frac{1}{2}) \\approx$ {{field-1}},\n$y(1) \\approx$ {{field-2}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186222018 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1+\\frac{1}{2})}{5} \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=0.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y+{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(0.6+1)}{5} \\\\  & =0.64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈0.60,\\,y(1)≈0.64.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220763 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186222020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220767 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186222020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314111 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314246 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222020 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186222020 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2