ons, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}(0) & y_{2}(0) \\\\ y_{1}(1) & y_{2}(1)\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\nThe fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\nFor this BVP, we have $det(A) =$ {{field-3}}.\n\nTherefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186229718 1074 "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2m-3 & =0 \\\\ (m-3)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {e}^{3} & {e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={e}^{-1}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{4}}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186229721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228488 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186229721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314231 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186229721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186229721 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0,\\,y(-2)=0,\\,y(2)-4{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,-2\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186229721 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$y^{″} = 0, y(-2) = 0, y(2) - 4y^{′}(2) = 0,-2 \\le x \\le 2$\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A$.\n\nThe solution is $y = A \\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)$." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186229721 1074 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(-2)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2A+B=0\\,⇒\\,B=2A.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=Ax+2A=A(x+2).\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(2)-4{y}^{′}(2)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A-4A=0\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot (x+2),$ where $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186229737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228610 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186229737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314670 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186229737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186229737 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x ∈ [- 1, 1]$.\n\n$f(x) = \\frac{1}{x}, g(x) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0,h(x) = \\cos x$." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186229737 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & x\\ge 0,\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186229737 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=0$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ except at the point $x=0,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,1]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186229742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228652 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186229742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314597 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186229742 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186229742 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x ∈ [0, ∞)$.\n\n$f(x) = {-1,\\begin{vmatrix}⌊x⌋is even \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}⌊x⌋is odd,g(x) = u(x - 2), h(x) = \\csc x$.\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌊x⌋$ is the floor function." 13194139533417 17592186045585 false] [17592186229742 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & ⌊x⌋\\text{ is even} \\\\ 1,\\, & ⌊x⌋\\text{ is odd},\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)=u(x-2),\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌊x⌋$ is the floor function." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186229742 1074 "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,2,3,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/08/084c469b225d2a4cc4bc148fa3a42fb1501141d1adcd91d8d37999409a8d313f.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the point $x=2,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}h(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}h(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\"" 1319413