4702 1075 #edb/ref 17592186312953 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314702 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314702 1077 "field-1" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314702 1079 #edb/ref 17592186312537 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314702 1080 #uuid "ff7d1035-2f98-5697-b98c-c8fadffe3983" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314703 1075 #edb/ref 17592186313930 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314703 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314703 1077 "field-1" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314703 1079 #edb/ref 17592186313930 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314703 1080 #uuid "fff9ae42-05ed-5c15-9feb-e04d24b0b620" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314704 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314278 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314704 1069 "q-338528" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314704 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314704 1071 "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y={csc}^{3}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=2{cos}^{2}⁡2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314704 1072 #uuid "280f32a1-77b0-558e-a146-5c2de5be7ce0" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314704 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={csc}^{3}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡2x+2{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {csc}^{2}⁡2x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{{sin}^{3}⁡2x}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then $du=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,dx,$ so $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\,du,$ and the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{{sin}^{3}⁡2x}\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,du) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{-3}\\,du \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{u}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{sin}^{2}⁡2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(-\\frac{1}{8{sin}^{2}⁡2x})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2x}{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}-\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}(2{cos}^{2}⁡2x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,+\\,{{\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314705 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314559 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314705 1069 "q-338531" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314705 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314705 1071 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x},\\,y(0)=1,\\,y(2)=0$\n- ${y}^{″}+4y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-1$\n- ${y}^{″}-y={x}^{2}$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314705 1072 #uuid "dabe6837-4031-5ea3-85c8-7a53d65944dd" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314705 1074 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314328 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314706 1069 "q-338533" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314706 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314706 1071 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=2,\\,y(5)=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+y={x}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(-3)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-3)=1$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314706 1072 #uuid "ce074393-6c5e-5e9b-ab9a-92fd971d7d06" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314706 1074 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-3$)." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314305 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314707 1069 "q-338539" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314707 1071 "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+y=x{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=3,\\,y(4)=3$\n- ${y}^{″}-y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0,\\,y(-2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(-2)=0$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314707 1072 #uuid "2fcfd17f-1887-5fc2-a463-d8fe70a76b3c" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314707 1074 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=4$).\n\n- The second system is not a boundary value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the *same* location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-2$). This is an **initial value problem.**" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314381 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314708 1069 "q-338555" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314708 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314708 1071 "The solution to the equation $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314708 1072 #uuid "0510a0f2-edd5-5099-af52-57bc8c455ec8" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314708 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-{90}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-{90}^{∘}$ is not in the given domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={270}^{∘}\n$$\n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx={270}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that \n$x={270}^{∘}$\nis the only solution." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314709 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314425 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314709 1069 "q-33857" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314709 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314709 1071 "Which integral  expression gives the arc length of the curve $f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,{t}^{2}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=1?$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314709 1072 #uuid "732ad1b9-5772-5700-8754-f590c45d555d" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314709 1074 "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{t}^{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314302 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314652 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314710 1069 "q-338573" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314710 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314710 1071 "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\sqrt{2}=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314710 1072 #uuid "d4361090-b0ba-502a-8af2-2c9398f9d515" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314710 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${135}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x={135}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/91/91e4de557177693203cbbc48c12bbf7af3de1739c8405ac05bdfa06d3e92638c.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x={-135}^{∘}$ and $x={135}^{∘}.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314409 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314577 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314711 1069 "q-338574" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314711 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314711 1071 "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314711 1072 #uuid "03c93438-78d6-56ca-99d6-08e8278edf47" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314711 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a22dba264faa711352724f135d2ec5a074202c4b5069dee2389fe954419a3770.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314501 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314669 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314712 1069 "q-338580" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314712 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{2},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314712 1072 #uuid "43e75940-ea81-5b28-a9f4-6ca4e79d3794" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314712 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{2}{3})=-0.729\\,727…≈-0.7297\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add $2\\pi :$ \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-0.7297+2\\pi ≈5.5535\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}=5.5535.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.7297.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.7297.$\n\n![](images/48/48d92d2bf48234d7c7aad44936cdefd97f02804383cb7ced2f838661a8c4922a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.7297 \\\\  & ≈3.8713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈3.9,\\,x≈5.6.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314064 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314421 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314713 1069 "q-338585" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314713 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{3},$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314713 1072 #uuid "9df329f0-689c-5480-af86-9fabef73edcc" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314713 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{3}{4})={(-48.590\\,377…)}^{∘}≈-{48.5904}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{48.5904}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={311.4096}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}={311.4096}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={48.5904}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={48.5904}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/d3/d319d2ea403460a3b4d86c837a560fd5188476c425399145bb911e1414b0a6f5.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{48.5904}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{228.5904}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{228.6}^{∘},\\,x≈{311.4}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314187 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314714 1069 "q-338595" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314714 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314714 1071 "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\,y(0)=1,\\,y(2)=0$\n- $3{y}^{″}-y={e}^{x},\\,y(1)=4$\n- ${y}^{″}+y={x}^{2},\\,y(-1)=2,\\,y(1)=2$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314714 1072 #uuid "f4fd4473-8c46-5808-aedb-c06540dd956d" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314714 1074 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$).\n\n- The second system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition.\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=1$)." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314715 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314327 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314715 1069 "q-338599" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314715 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314715 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0,\\,y(-4)=0,\\,y(0)-4{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,-4\\le x\\le 0\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314715 1072 #uuid "2c3e72e0-598a-5bc9-b8c5-a7b7871ad697" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314715 1074 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(-4)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-4A+B=0\\,⇒\\,B=4A.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=Ax+4A=A(x+4).\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(0)-4{y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A-4A=0\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot (x+4),$ where $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314062 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314716 1069 "q-338602" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314716 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+4y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\,y(-1)=2,\\,y(3)=-1,\\,-1\\le x\\le 3\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314716 1072 #uuid "75524188-4a90-56dd-9382-645dc560d528" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314716 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a)=y(b),\\,{y}^{′}(a)={y}^{′}(b)\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x=a=-1$ and $x=b=3.$\n\n$$\ny(-1)=2,\\,y(3)=-1\n$$\n\nThese are Dirichlet boundary conditions." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314456 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314717 1069 "q-338604" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314717 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314717 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)-3{y}^{′}(x)+2y(x)={e}^{x},\\,y(0)=1,\\,y(2)=5,\\,0\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314717 1072 #uuid "4086afd3-4656-531a-82a0-d50ed2984b51" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314717 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a)=y(b),\\,{y}^{′}(a)={y}^{′}(b)\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x=a=0$ and $x=b=2.$\n\n$$\ny(0)=1,\\,y(2)=5\n$$\n\nThese are Dirichlet boundary conditions." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314561 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314718 1069 "q-338612" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314718 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)-y(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(5)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 5\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314718 1072 #uuid "177b521d-a314-54a6-9616-ef1fbfe38688" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314718 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a)=y(b),\\,{y}^{′}(a)={y}^{′}(b)\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x=a=1$ and $x=b=5.$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(1)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(5)=0\n$$\n\nThese are Neumann boundary conditions." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314106 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314719 1069 "q-338614" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314719 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+9y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(3)=4,\\,0\\le x\\le 3\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314719 1072 #uuid "64554674-0bb3-58e1-bc66-00294a471a41" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314719 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a)=y(b),\\,{y}^{′}(a)={y}^{′}(b)\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x=a=0$ and $x=b=3.$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(3)=4\n$$\n\nThese are Neumann boundary conditions." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314537 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314720 1069 "q-338616" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314720 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314720 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{y}^{′}(x)={x}^{3},\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-3,\\,-1\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314720 1072 #uuid "af32759f-057f-566e-a72f-923823a621d7" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314720 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a)=y(b),\\,{y}^{′}(a)={y}^{′}(b)\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x=a=-1$ and $x=b=2.$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(-1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-3\n$$\n\nThese are Neumann boundary conditions." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314533 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314721 1069 "q-338618" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314721 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+4y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,2y(-2)-{y}^{′}(-2)=3,\\,y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=-1,\\,-2\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314721 1072 #uuid "d93ebbab-ee2f-5876-8ddf-4888afe163a7" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314721 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a)=y(b),\\,{y}^{′}(a)={y}^{′}(b)\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x=a=-2$ and $x=b=1.$\n\n$$\n2y(-2)-{y}^{′}(-2)=3,\\,y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=-1\n$$\n\nThese are Robin (mixed) boundary conditions." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314722 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314443 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314722 1069 "q-338645" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314722 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314722 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)-4y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-4y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314722 1072 #uuid "a83efc62-7398-5466-b65f-5821c4945754" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314722 1074 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4.$ So, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4A{e}^{4x}-4B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)-4y(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4A-4B)-4(A+B) & =0 \\\\ -8B & =0 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-4y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A{e}^{2}-4A{e}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot {e}^{4x},$ where $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314342 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314723 1069 "q-338657" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314723 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314723 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314723 1072 #uuid "cf23a663-807f-5c1f-82e3-56863f8dad5c" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314723 1074 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314724 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314569 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314724 1069 "q-338675" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314724 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314724 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314724 1072 #uuid "af4eccce-a74f-5bb3-b316-f577c07564aa" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314724 1074 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+2)=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314725 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314674 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314725 1069 "q-338676" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314725 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314725 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}-2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314725 1072 #uuid "c4ef0cfb-cd70-5ecd-8dbc-77f3eae090ec" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314725 1074 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x/4}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}-2)=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314282 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314368 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314726 1069 "q-338688" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314726 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314726 1071 "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=0$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314726 1072 #uuid "c87c5173-e872-5d2d-993e-17bcdc6ae00d" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314726 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/8e/8ea1a5ca6b0dc0bd7867ab841c629f9966f2b97de4585b9c595a33af206c4a7f.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314075 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314094 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314394 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314651 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314727 1069 "q-338695" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314727 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314727 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+x{y}^{′}(x)+(1+{x}^{2})y(x)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n$$\ny(0)=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314727 1072 #uuid "4c856911-b2c8-57b4-a878-13a738162f76" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314727 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+x{y}^{′}(x)+(1+{x}^{2})y(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)≡0,$ the equation is $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\ny(0)=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, and the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{homogeneous}.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314169 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314236 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314514 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314667 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314728 1069 "q-338698" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314728 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314728 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+(x+1){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0,\\,-2\\le x\\le 0\n$$\n\n$$\ny(-2)=0,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314728 1072 #uuid "2410f4e7-2ccf-526a-9aee-67a97f39587e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314728 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+(x+1){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)≡0,$ the equation is $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\ny(-2)=0,\\,y(0)=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, and the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{homogeneous}.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314276 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314356 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314729 1069 "q-338710" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314729 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314729 1071 "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **the nearest integer**, the solutions of the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314729 1072 #uuid "863434bc-9880-5d8f-880f-7fef27155e0c" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314729 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{5}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{5}{8})=2.245\\,927…≈2.246\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.246$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is $x=2.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.896.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.896.$\n\n![](images/99/992325c7cbf34f4b981af0a9f34740990d39694e727861ca92bf591037436327.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.896 \\\\  & ≈4.038\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =4.038-2\\pi  \\\\  & ≈-2.245 \\\\  & ≈-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314029 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314321 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314551 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314644 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314730 1069 "q-338714" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314730 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314730 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+x{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)={x}^{2}+1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\n$$\ny(0)=1,\\,y(\\pi )=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314730 1072 #uuid "099c7d35-9743-59c2-a454-5854620973b9" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314730 1074 "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+x{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)={x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)={x}^{2}+1≢0,$ the equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\ny(0)=1,\\,y(\\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is inhomogeneous, and the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314022 13194139533417 1759218