ogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,{y}^{′}(x)+2y(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)={e}^{x}≢0,$ the equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-3.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Neumann}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is inhomogeneous, and the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314732 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314414 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314732 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314629 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314732 1069 "q-338723" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314732 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314732 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3=-2,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314732 1072 #uuid "57e3ccf9-17c1-5add-96a9-fe13c7b364fe" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314732 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3 & =-2 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-5 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{4}{5})≈-0.9273\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+2\\pi 