alent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/fd/fd0cf34edebb21a059d60bb3588b27df2da4c86de0bdca044c0e702d9e239d7b.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\nx={60}^{∘},\\,x={120}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314745 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314240 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314745 1069 "q-338817" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314745 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314745 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314745 1072 #uuid "64d5c17a-f385-5f9e-987c-dea9b357b781" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314745 1074 "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with \n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/e1/e164233abcdf9062fd856a100fb7b792912fa030746a63f01ca7fd6683bf7f09.png)" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314746 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314293 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314746 1069 "q-338818" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314746 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314746 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314746 1072 #uuid "9ccdea50-cae6-504b-8744-6857f8671d1e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314746 1074 "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with \n\n$$\n|4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 4.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{2}$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/fd/fd6dbbe8be077ed2eda955802f112540aa8aea73c5c65bae4f0f034176bbdccd.png)" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314747 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314406 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314747 1069 "q-338820" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314747 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314747 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x+{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314747 1072 #uuid "59588060-2b04-570f-81b9-c2859d3add6e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314747 1074 "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with \n\n$$\n|-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $4x+{x}^{2}$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x+{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x+{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/dd/dd869f9140a12c19a0672963c064a4581767a3e39fac8c49b76b6e106755080f.png)" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314748 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314232 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314748 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314251 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314748 1069 "q-338870" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314748 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314748 1071 "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314748 1072 #uuid "3e13738f-f5b5-5418-9627-22b39e555f1e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314748 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x,y) & ={e}^{-\\int 2/y\\text{ }\\text{d}y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}({x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ {{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(-1)(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}+y)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-y.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314280 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314749 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314478 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314749 1069 "q-338900" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314749 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314749 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16\\lambda {y}^{′}+64{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314749 1072 #uuid "9b441f0c-d5e0-5b6a-9f79-45716a07debc" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314749 1074 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16\\lambda m+64{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-8\\lambda )(m-8\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=8\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{8\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{8\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{8\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda x}+B{e}^{8\\lambda x}+8B\\lambda x{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (8\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(8A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+16A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(8\\lambda )A+B=0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+16\\lambda  & 3+16\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda (3+16\\lambda )-(1)(1+16\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 24\\lambda +128{\\lambda }^{2}-1-16\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 128{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (16\\lambda -1)(8\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x