 [17592186314756 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{3}^{x}+5}{{2}^{x}-3}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314756 1072 #uuid "568ff4f8-9107-5a4c-94d8-86c780b87889" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314756 1074 "We can evaluate this limit using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{3}^{x}+5}{{2}^{x}-3} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({3}^{x}+5)}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}({2}^{x}-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}+5}{{2}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+5}{4-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{1} \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314757 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314273 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314757 1069 "q-339080" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314757 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314757 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {7}^{-x})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314757 1072 #uuid "5553df31-6113-5f5b-a73a-06eefe35a840" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314757 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {7}^{-x}) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({7}^{-1})}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/f2/f2858b7d6adc07c0f2196bbe8170f17fa02aff19d494726c08f7c03a419afd53.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function approaches zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314142 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314214 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314543 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314758 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314646 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314758 1069 "q-339097" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314758 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314758 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0,\\,y(0)=y(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(\\pi ) & {y}_{2}(\\pi )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$  {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314758 1072 #uuid "a430fd57-63d6-5c8d-a2e1-572f77f00d5f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314758 1074 "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=\\pi ,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(\\pi ) & {y}_{2}(\\pi )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we solve the auxiliary equation\n\n$$\n{m}^{2}+1=0,\n$$\n\nwhich has roots $\\pm i.$ Thus, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =0(-1)-1(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has infinitely many solutions.}$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314566 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314759 1069 "q-33913" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314759 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314759 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(1,-1,1)$, $P(2,0,3)$ and $Q(1,1,0).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314759 1072 #uuid "686d05b3-6ae6-58de-961c-a5d4a934cbc6" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314759 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-1,1⟩,\\,p=⟨2,0,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,3⟩-⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩-⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-5i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-5,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =⟨2,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =-4 \\\\ -5x+y+2z & =-4 \\\\ -5x+y+2z+4 & =0 \\\\ 5x-y-2z-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314611 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314760 1069 "q-339131" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314760 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314760 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(2\\sqrt{3}x+6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314760 1072 #uuid "4b46ef53-9e9f-5f4a-9061-327f429a33fa" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314760 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](images/85/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$   \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(2\\sqrt{3}x+6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =2\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+6\\cdot \\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3+6\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314761 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314289 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314761 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314463 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314761 1069 "q-339140" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314761 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314761 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{5}{x}y=7,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below:\n\n$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314761 1072 #uuid "f406ffe6-dbd4-575f-96ea-c90a9a6d9f11" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314761 1074 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{5}{x}$ and $Q(x)=7.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (5/x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5})} \\\\  & ={x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{5}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{5}{x}y) & ={x}^{5}\\cdot 7 \\\\ {x}^{5}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5{x}^{4}y & =7{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{5})\\,dx & =\\int 7{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{5} & =\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{7x}{6}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314762 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314579 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314762 1069 "q-339219" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314762 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314762 1071 "The price $P(t)$ of a particular good varies according to the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=rP(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in years, and $r>0.$ It is known that the price of the good tripled after the first six years. How long did it take for the price to double from the initial price?" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314762 1072 #uuid "b71a4a6b-7596-5bb0-a385-748571c7e195" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314762 1074 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}{e}^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the price of the good when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the price equals $2{P}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =2{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}}=2{P}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =2{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =2\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ r{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the price of the good tripled after the first six years. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(6)=3{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{6r}=3{P}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{6r} & =3{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{6r} & =3\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{6r} & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{6r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & ≈3.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314763 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314124 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314763 1069 "q-339223" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314763 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314763 1071 "The population $P(t)$ of mice in a forest varies according to the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=rP(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in months from the start of the observation period, and $r>0.$  It is known that the mouse population quintuples (increases fivefold) after the first two months. How long does it take for the mouse population to increase eightfold from the start of the observation period? Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314763 1072 #uuid "30f450e1-9f50-50d0-a5b8-9d9411257241" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314763 1074 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}{e}^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the mouse population when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the mouse population equals $8{P}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =8{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}}=8{P}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =8{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =8\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =8 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ r{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the population quintuples after the first two months. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(2)=5{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{2r}=5{P}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{2r} & =5{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{2r} & =5\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{2r} & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\  & ≈2.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314764 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314133 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314764 1069 "q-339228" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314764 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314764 1071 "A car is traveling along a straight road to a particular destination. The remaining distance $D(t)$ varies according to the differential equation ${D}^{′}(t)=-rD(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in hours, and $r>0.$ Two hours into the journey, the remaining distance is three-quarters of the journey's total distance. How long does it take for the remaining distance to reduce to one-third of the journey's total distance? Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314764 1072 #uuid "f3648d5b-2812-593c-b336-3a42fac9c75a" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314764 1074 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nD(t)={D}_{0}{e}^{-rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${D}_{0}$ is the distance when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the distance equals $\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({t}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{D}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}}=\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{D}_{0}}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cancel{{D}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ -r{t}_{1} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that two hours into the journey, the remaining distance is three-quarters of the journey's total distance. Therefore,\n\n$$\nD(2)=\\frac{3}{4}{D}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{D}_{0}{e}^{-2r}=\\frac{3}{4}{D}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{0}{e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{3}{4}{D}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{D}_{0}}{e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cancel{{D}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-2r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\ -2r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)}{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)} \\\\  & ≈7.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314158 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314765 1069 "q-339235" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314765 1071 "The number of subscribers $S(t)$ to a new video streaming service after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation ${S}^{′}(t)=0.11S(t).$ If the streaming service had $60\\,000$ subscribers after $20$ weeks, calculate the number of subscribers after $32$ weeks." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314765 1072 #uuid "61e72b24-d234-5774-a24e-6716abb34347" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314765 1074 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.11t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial number of subscribers.\n\nWe are given that after $20$ weeks, $60\\,000$ people subscribed to the service. So, substituting $t=20$ and $S(20)=60\\,000$ into our general solution and solving for ${S}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(20) & ={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.11\\cdot 20} \\\\ 60\\,000 & ={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{2.2} \\\\ {S}_{0} & =\\frac{60\\,000}{{e}^{2.2}} \\\\  & ≈6\\,648.190,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nS(t)=6\\,648.190\\,{e}^{0.11t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the number of subscribers after $32$ weeks. So, substituting $t=32$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(32) & =6\\,648.190\\,{e}^{0.11\\cdot 32} \\\\  & =6\\,648.190\\,{e}^{3.52} \\\\  & ≈224\\,605,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest whole number. \n\nTherefore, the streaming service will have about $224\\,605$ subscribers after $32$ weeks." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314261 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314766 1069 "q-339237" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314766 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314766 1071 "The population $P(t)$ of rabbits in a forest after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=0.11P(t).$ If there were $786$ rabbits after $8$ weeks, calculate the rabbit population after $15$ weeks." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314766 1072 #uuid "dde71f8f-bba2-5206-a37a-f8c5e12abf05" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314766 1074 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.11t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial number of rabbits.\n\nWe are given that after $8$ weeks, there were $786$ rabbits. So, substituting $t=8$ and $P(8)=786$ into our general solution and solving for ${P}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(8) & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.11\\cdot 8} \\\\ 786 & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.88} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{786}{{e}^{0.88}} \\\\  & ≈326.019,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=326.019\\,{e}^{0.11t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the number of rabbits after $15$ weeks. So, substituting $t=15$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(15) & =326.019\\,{e}^{0.11\\cdot 15} \\\\  & =326.019\\,{e}^{1.65} \\\\  & ≈1\\,698,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest whole number. \n\nTherefore, there will be about $1\\,698$ rabbits after $15$ weeks." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314767 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314085 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314767 1069 "q-339238" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314767 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314767 1071 "The value $P(t)$ of a particular luxury car $t$ years after the year $2010$ can be modeled by the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=0.025P(t).$ If the car's value in $2016$ was $$62\\,000,$ calculate its value in the year $2025.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314767 1072 #uuid "433e9fa5-4fc4-562a-b02f-bef18204002b" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314767 1074 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.025t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial value of the car.\n\nWe are given that the value in $2016$ was $$62\\,000.$ So, substituting $t=6$ and $P(6)=$62\\,000$ into our general solution and solving for ${P}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6) & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.025\\cdot 6} \\\\ 62\\,000 & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.15} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{62\\,000}{{e}^{0.15}} \\\\  & ≈53\\,363.895,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=53\\,363.895\\,{e}^{0.025t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the car's value $15$ years after $2010.$ So, substituting $t=15$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(15) & =53\\,363.895\\,{e}^{0.025\\cdot 15} \\\\  & =53\\,363.895\\,{e}^{0.375} \\\\  & ≈77\\,644,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest whole number. \n\nTherefore, the car's value in $2025$ will be $$77\\,644.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314768 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314464 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314768 1069 "q-339239" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314768 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314768 1071 "The population $P(t)$ of fish in a pond after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=0.09P(t).$ If the fish population was $8\\,000$ after $18$ weeks, calculate the fish population after $23$ weeks." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314768 1072 #uuid "c1343f64-d830-54e8-a05f-16c3f85806cb" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314768 1074 "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.09t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial fish population.\n\nWe are given that after $18$ weeks, the population was $8\\,000.$ So, substituting $t=18$ and $P(18)=8\\,000$ into our general solution and solving for ${P}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(18) & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.09\\cdot 18} \\\\ 8\\,000 & ={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{1.62} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{8\\,000}{{e}^{1.62}} \\\\  & ≈1\\,583.190,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nHence, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=1\\,583.190\\,{e}^{0.09t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the fish population after $23$ weeks. So, substituting $t=23$ into our particular solution, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(23) & =1\\,583.190\\,{e}^{0.09\\cdot 23} \\\\  & =1\\,583.190\\,{e}^{2.07} \\\\  & ≈12\\,547,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest whole number. \n\nTherefore, approximately $12\\,547$ fish will be in the pond after $23$ weeks." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314026 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314769 1069 "q-339253" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314769 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314769 1071 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-y=4x$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-5y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(2)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=3$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6x{y}^{′}+9y={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(5)=-2$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314769 1072 #uuid "baccee23-0e1a-561a-a3ca-cf008d4fd658" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314769 1074 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=2$).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=5$). This is a **boundary value problem.**" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314270 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314770 1069 "q-339254" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314770 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314770 1071 "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x,\\,y(-1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=0$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y=8$\n- $x{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-3y={e}^{x},\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314770 1072 #uuid "99d74bf8-bcac-5e65-b877-61e885b1b024" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314770 1074 "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem.  It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$)." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314557 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314771 1069 "q-33926" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314771 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314771 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,0,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨2,0,1⟩.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314771 1072 #uuid "2229970c-380d-5c52-9385-f1cac1675d7c" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314771 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,0,3⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨2,0,1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =⟨1,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ & =5 \\\\ 2x+z & =5 \\\\ 2x+z-5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314391 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314772 1069 "q-339264" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314772 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314772 1071 "![](images/db/db123092734fe2d84dacd2da2d005d001aadb673d1ad7f30c9527ef9f5a8ba7c.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314772 1072 #uuid "1e42cd63-b546-5e09-911a-13a51ccd623a" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314772 1074 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-1)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-1,3].$ Using the additive property of integrals, we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-1).$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,3].$ The corresponding region is a semicircle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =8+2\\pi  \\\\  & =8+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314044 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314773 1069 "q-339266" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314773 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314773 1071 "![](images/f4/f418496b3460fddc13ea2beb4c099e97ed4d2fa1d89e04c01862d142e4d4177f.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314773 1072 #uuid "1cd24383-d39c-50dd-a8ed-f34f040da0ee" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314773 1074 "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-6,-3),$ the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in (-3,3),$ and the line segment for $x\\in (3,6].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-6,-3).$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-3,3).$ The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle of radius $3$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh+\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =6(1)+\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (3)^{2} \\\\  & =6+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (3,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+6+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =18+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314235 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314774 1069 "q-339297" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314774 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)-y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)-y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314774 1072 #uuid "304e6de3-46cb-5fa9-ac57-f62fbb8abf23" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314774 1074 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 1.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A{e}^{x}-B{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)-y(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-B-(A+B) & =0 \\\\ -2B & =0 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(1)-y(1)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nA{e}^{1}-A{e}^{1}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is\n\n$$\ny=A\\cdot {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314496 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314775 1069 "q-339311" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314775 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314775 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Suppose that $X∼N(\\mu ,{5.5}^{2})$ and $P(X\\le 18.2)=0.5871.$ Find $\\mu .$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314775 1072 #uuid "3e75bdea-4506-5a3d-8157-9fb9c7f2fd37" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314775 1074 "First, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 18.2) & =0.5871 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5}) & =0.5871 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5}) & =0.5871 \\\\ \\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5871)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.22)=0.5871\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.5871)=0.22.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5} & =0.22 \\\\ 18.2-\\mu  & =1.21 \\\\ \\mu  & =16.99.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314033 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314776 1069 "q-339328" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314776 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314776 1071 "![](images/dc/dcc3b282b99d1b74669ea47455f424361258e33497527bd69e29d50c5083aec4.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314776 1072 #uuid "5cfe8374-e5f6-5ffc-8dc4-472929c56e02" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314776 1074 "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-3),$ the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (-3,-1),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [-1,4].$ Using the additive property of integrals, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-3).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-3,-1).$ The corresponding region is a combination of an inverted quarter-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=\\pi -4.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,4].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-4+\\pi -4+10 \\\\  & =2+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314572 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314777 1069 "q-339330" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314777 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }(\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314777 1072 #uuid "821c3a6e-2859-5147-bbd0-a91716f2cba7" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314777 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1):$\n\n![](images/e4/e441953c2cefc6f817a64cb7ce0ebce53164ebb4d3974ae871b14d57450b5167.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }(\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1))=\\pi -(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314068 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314778 1069 "q-339358" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314778 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314778 1071 "Consider the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t).$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314778 1072 #uuid "4e3423d0-f510-58c9-aea1-a58d5e9caea0" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314778 1074 "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t)^{′} \\\\ (5t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(2{t}^{2})}^{′} \\\\ {(2t-8)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{({e}^{2t})}^{′} \\\\ {({e}^{t})}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{3}(t)$ only\"." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314477 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314779 1069 "q-339359" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314779 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314779 1071 "Consider the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t).$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314779 1072 #uuid "4511edb4-579c-5286-add9-1c28bfe4202a" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314779 1074 "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{({e}^{-t})}^{′} \\\\ {(0)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(0)}^{′} \\\\ {({e}^{2t})}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(t)}^{′} \\\\ {(t+1)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 2(t+1)\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ only\"." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314627 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314780 1069 "q-339369" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314780 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314780 1071 "If the following system of differential equations is written in the form ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then what is the matrix $A?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+3{x}_{1}(t)-5{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314780 1072 #uuid "b7edba35-a010-57da-a58a-e69d5c024b87" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314780 1074 "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a(t) & b(t) \\\\ c(t) & d(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ g(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+3{x}_{1}(t)-5{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-5{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314378 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314781 1069 "q-339372" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314781 1071 "If the following system of differential equations is written in the form ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then what is the matrix $A?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)+5{x}_{1}(t)-9{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314781 1072 #uuid "56f9256c-a1cb-5ec7-9745-959282a76bb8" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314781 1074 "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n