hat\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5))=2\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314190 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314783 1069 "q-339406" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314783 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314783 1072 #uuid "14adae41-e106-531a-960b-9ce7071cabb3" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314783 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x} & =(-\\frac{5}{3})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/9a/9a249437f97a441c409b17e0f30a59bdcb70df02610f18d9320361d3d0d6708a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{5}{3})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}=(-\\frac{5}{3})\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314423 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314784 1069 "q-339407" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314784 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314784 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314784 1072 #uuid "5ff15497-b2ec-532a-981c-c68f8be3bb35" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314784 1074 "We recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{8})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/99/99c2329c99005af33f4b62d61547e18dcaa7f04afc931df57439bd1ca08ff35c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314353 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314785 1069 "q-339411" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314785 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314785 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2\\cdot {e}^{4x-3}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314785 1072 #uuid "3d1152e8-3cad-5779-a018-ff89eaa712bd" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314785 1074 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2\\cdot {e}^{4x-3} & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{4x-3} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{-3}\\cdot {e}^{4x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {e}^{-3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{4x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={({e}^{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/65/6590b80777c49b3d018fa38ce79c7fb14103365f783dac18135b8d3637ee724d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe negative factor $-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}=-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot \\infty =-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314146 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314786 1069 "q-339413" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314786 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314786 1072 #uuid "eafbfa31-bdcf-5ba4-9143-c9963f01a13a" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314786 1074 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({5}^{3}\\cdot {5}^{-2x}) \\\\  & ={5}^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{-2x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({5}^{-2})}^{x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{{5}^{2}})}^{x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/37/3795890dac6f542c8aa75d61deb8a03192a41532270b74eafcd972e82ac6721e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x}=125\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314403 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314787 1069 "q-339417" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314787 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{2-3x})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314787 1072 #uuid "397e8c27-583b-5dc5-be7b-21bdc9ee9f71" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314787 1074 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{2-3x}) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2-3x} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{-3x} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{({3}^{-3})}^{x} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}})}^{x} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{27})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{27})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/60/60c4ef99a2617fd076e6350ba68e14311ebc41b9b0f33fcc07b871198dfc103d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{27})}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{27})}^{x}=(-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot \\infty =-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314440 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314788 1069 "q-339421" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314788 1071 "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{4{x}^{6}-5x}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314788 1072 #uuid "530ff544-ed9d-585e-bfc3-0dae9081b382" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314788 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{6}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{4{x}^{6}-5x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}})}{(\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}})}{(4-\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{4-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314374 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314789 1069 "q-339426" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314789 1071 "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3-x-8{x}^{2}}{k\\,{x}^{2}+7x-4}=\\frac{2}{5},$  what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314789 1072 #uuid "76e07179-8131-59be-8696-de3b1d10efb1" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314789 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3-x-8{x}^{2}}{k\\,{x}^{2}+7x-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{\\,{x}^{2}}+\\frac{7x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}-8)}{(k+\\frac{7}{x}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0-8}{k+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{k} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{k} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 2k & =-40 \\\\ k & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314431 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314790 1069 "q-339462" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314790 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={x}^{5}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314790 1072 #uuid "d57eb0df-b672-57ca-8fc9-d0cc8d741df8" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314790 1074 "The function $f(x)$  is the sum of two power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4/3}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{(5-1)}+\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{(4/3-1)} \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{1/3} \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314127 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314791 1069 "q-339475" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314791 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314791 1072 #uuid "de7a329f-b60f-52c7-b160-7260cba30d3d" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314791 1074 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm \\text{i}.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\\,⇒\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ where $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314335 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314792 1069 "q-339477" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314792 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314792 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314792 1072 #uuid "f1b72358-e12f-522e-a6d1-8b47c27da23b" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314792 1074 "![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314061 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314793 1069 "q-339490" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314793 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314793 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314793 1072 #uuid "dced79c7-5f2c-5235-8004-8d45b7bc9bd7" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314793 1074 "![](images/68/68f1ebd0457e0c127f8ed6c979feba58196aa6bb43d97d211efa9b39bc33f637.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314794 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314384 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314794 1069 "q-339500" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314794 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314794 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1))=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314794 1072 #uuid "82116abe-5463-5037-8e4c-b820d9de02f0" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314794 1074 "![](images/f5/f5cef3bda76e80edfa467a90823c9b6ad6aca1f8a5e8e3e06dc585258e975e0a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314795 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314295 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314795 1069 "q-339507" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314795 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314795 1071 "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|-12x+5|}{4x+3}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314795 1072 #uuid "e3551f74-1b2f-52cf-ac61-38bcccfdf9b3" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314795 1074 "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|-12x+5|=-12x+5.$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{|-12x+5|}{4x+3}=\\frac{-12x+5}{4x+3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12x+5}{4x+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-12x}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-12+\\frac{5}{x})}{(4+\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12+0}{4+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314582 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314796 1069 "q-339531" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314796 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314796 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{e}^{x}$  has a root in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314796 1072 #uuid "b8a942f2-7fad-5e85-9ffb-0a3510bab085" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314796 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(2{x}^{3}-{e}^{x})^{′}=6{x}^{2}-{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}}{6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}})-(2{x}_{n}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{n}})}{6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}{e}^{{x}_{n}}+{e}^{{x}_{n}}}{6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{4{x}_{0}^{3}-{x}_{0}{e}^{{x}_{0}}+{e}^{{x}_{0}}}{6{x}_{0}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1)^{3}-(1){e}^{1}+{e}^{1}}{6(1)^{2}-{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =1.218\\,873….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{4{x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}}+{e}^{{x}_{1}}}{6{x}_{1}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.218\\,873…)^{3}-(1.218\\,873…){e}^{1.218\\,873…}+{e}^{1.218\\,873…}}{6(1.218\\,873…)^{2}-{e}^{1.218\\,873…}} \\\\  & =1.175\\,790…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{4{x}_{2}^{3}-{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{2}}+{e}^{{x}_{2}}}{6{x}_{2}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.175\\,790…)^{3}-(1.175\\,790…){e}^{1.175\\,790…}+{e}^{1.175\\,790…}}{6(1.175\\,790…)^{2}-{e}^{1.175\\,790…}} \\\\  & =1.173\\,749…, \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{4{x}_{3}^{3}-{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{3}}+{e}^{{x}_{3}}}{6{x}_{3}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.173\\,749…)^{3}-(1.173\\,749…){e}^{1.173\\,749…}+{e}^{1.173\\,749…}}{6(1.173\\,749…)^{2}-{e}^{1.173\\,749…}} \\\\  & =1.173\\,744…,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.174$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.174$ are the same when rounded to three decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.174,$ rounded to three decimal places." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314797 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314482 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314797 1069 "q-339539" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314797 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314797 1071 "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}^{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\,{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)+3t\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314797 1072 #uuid "27876a51-c9f8-5f4d-9c9b-be85b540294f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314797 1074 "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}, &  & b(t)=-2, &  & f(t)=1, \\\\ c(t)=\\sqrt{7}, &  & d(t)=5, &  & g(t)=3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314392 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314798 1069 "q-339540" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314798 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314798 1071 "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t)\\,{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+\\sqrt{3}\\,t\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}){x}_{1}(t)+{13}^{2}{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{1}(t)-\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}^{2}(t)+{x}_{2}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314798 1072 #uuid 