operatorname{ln}⁡(5))^{2}{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314801 1072 #uuid "91a61e63-0bab-516c-b096-3ec2dc905625" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314801 1074 "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=3, &  & b(t)=-8, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314802 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314552 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314802 1069 "q-339573" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314802 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314802 1071 "![](images/be/be0329f2bc3722a6b3381dcfdbed212dc1e647f7f165cefb4c00f939b74b6d74.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314802 1072 #uuid "3239873a-14d1-5840-a165-7209d138510f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314802 1074 "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-3,1),$ the line segment for $x\\in [1,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [3,6].$ Therefore, we can use the additive property of integrals to write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$   The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (2)^{2}+4(1) \\\\  & =2\\pi +4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$   The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,6].$   The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi +4+2-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi +3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314418 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314803 1069 "q-339585" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314803 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314803 1071 "If $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=10$ and $g(10)=5,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {10}^{+}}\\frac{g(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{3g(10)}=$\n {{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314803 1072 #uuid "10c39566-1794-5aaa-b056-02cead0ac83d" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314803 1074 "Since $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{+}}g(x)=g(10)=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{+}}\\frac{g(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{3g(10)} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{+}}g(x)-{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{+}}\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{3g(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-\\operatorname{log}⁡10}{3(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{3(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314525 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314804 1069 "q-339591" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314804 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314804 1071 "The mass of a radioactive sample $R(t)$ (in milligrams) after $t$ days can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-0.05R.\n$$\n\nIf the sample initially had a mass of $80$ milligrams, calculate its mass after $30$ days. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314804 1072 #uuid "2bc6ca00-9c74-5560-90c3-3edc0b06719c" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314804 1074 "We model the mass of the sample using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-0.05R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nR(t)={R}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${R}_{0}$ is the initial mass.\n\nWe're told that the initial mass of the sample is $80$ milligrams, so we have ${R}_{0}=80.$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the sample $R$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nR(t)=80\\,{e}^{-0.05t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the mass of the sample when $t=30,$ we substitute $t=30$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(30) & =80\\,{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 30} \\\\  & =80\\,{e}^{-1.5} \\\\  & =17.850…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the mass of the sample after $30$ days is $18$ milligrams." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314050 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314805 1069 "q-339592" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314805 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314805 1071 "If $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=5$ and $g(5)=5,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{g(x)-{x}^{2}}{4x}=$\n {{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314805 1072 #uuid "92a7df39-061c-5554-9a16-e4eca2c01a03" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314805 1074 "Since $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}g(x)=g(5)=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{g(x)-{x}^{2}}{4x} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}g(x)-\\text{ }{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}{x}^{2}}{{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-{5}^{2}}{4(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-25}{20} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314806 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314571 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314806 1069 "q-339597" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314806 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314806 1071 "If $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=10$ and $h(10)=15,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}(xh(x)-5h(10))=$\n {{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314806 1072 #uuid "22ad6b6e-2655-5a51-98a6-093bed4b5988" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314806 1074 "Since $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}h(x)=h(10)=15.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}(xh(x)-5h(10)) & ={lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}x\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}h(x)-5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {10}^{-}}h(10) \\\\  & =10\\cdot 15-5\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =150-75 \\\\  & =75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314807 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314164 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314807 1069 "q-339602" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314807 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314807 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)=x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2$ has a root in the interval $[0,1],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=0.1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314807 1072 #uuid "86dc4167-3401-5668-995a-2c45fdd8be71" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314807 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2)^{′}=-\\frac{1}{x}+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2}{(-\\frac{1}{{x}_{n}}+1)} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2)}{{x}_{n}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-1)-{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2)}{{x}_{n}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}+1)}{{x}_{n}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}_{0}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{0}+1)}{{x}_{0}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1\\,(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.1)+1)}{0.1-1} \\\\  & =0.144…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1}+1)}{{x}_{1}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.144…(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.144…)+1)}{0.144⋯-1} \\\\  & =0.157…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{{x}_{2}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{2}+1)}{{x}_{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.157…(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.157…)+1)}{0.157⋯-1} \\\\  & =0.158….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈0.16$ and ${x}_{3}≈0.16$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈0.16,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314808 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314460 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314808 1069 "q-339612" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314808 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314808 1071 "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+x+5}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314808 1072 #uuid "626d970f-3209-5977-874c-ff5fe2eb9780" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314808 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{6}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314672 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314809 1069 "q-339621" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314809 1071 "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{9}-6{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{3}+10x}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314809 1072 #uuid "e5fd476b-94fc-5bb9-8c11-525e44dceb8a" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314809 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{9}-6{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{3}+10x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{9}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{6}-\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\text{ }\\frac{-{x}^{6}-0+0}{1+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{6} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314154 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314810 1069 "q-339623" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314810 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314810 1071 "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314810 1072 #uuid "53a17b4c-4adf-5239-8971-3a7f7c6e95fd" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314810 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{5-0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314166 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314811 1069 "q-339629" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314811 1071 "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-2}{2{x}^{4}+3}$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314811 1072 #uuid "632f601c-e4fe-5306-bafd-fe80baef5799" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314811 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-2}{2{x}^{4}+3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(6+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+0+0}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=3.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314812 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314194 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314812 1069 "q-339641" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314812 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314812 1071 "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x>0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}?$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote is $y=$\n\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314812 1072 #uuid "1a3ec4d7-bd9a-5b1a-b0c1-e121685fdff5" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314812 1074 "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|3-8x|=8x-3.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x>0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8x}{x}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8-\\frac{3}{x})}{(4+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-0}{4+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314438 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314508 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314598 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314616 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314632 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314680 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314813 1069 "q-339645" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314813 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314813 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)-2t\\,{x}_{1}(t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)+5{x}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n| ${x}^{′}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,x(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314813 1072 #uuid "a8b0b675-d2c6-5bca-861c-bcc3cc2bcf16" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314813 1074 "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a(t) & b(t) \\\\ c(t) & d(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ g(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)-2t\\,{x}_{1}(t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)+5{x}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2t\\,{x}_{1}(t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,{x}_{2}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-5{x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2t & 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314814 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314059 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314814 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314188 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314814 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314206 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314814 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314339 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314814 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314494 13194139533417 17