m as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314182 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314209 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314286 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314287 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314595 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314628 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314815 1069 "q-339648" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314815 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314815 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+4{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)=t \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n| ${x}^{′}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,x(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314815 1072 #uuid "c1a120a0-53b9-5165-81bd-feeb29ddd094" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314815 1074 "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a(t) & b(t) \\\\ c(t) & d(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ g(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+4{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)=t \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+t \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314816 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314614 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314816 1069 "q-339650" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314816 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314816 1071 "The population of a bacterial culture $N=N(t)$ after $t$ hours can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If the culture initially contained $500$ bacteria, and $N(3)=2\\,000,$ calculate the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314816 1072 #uuid "5a9b5944-9f23-58ad-9832-a16d905d27ec" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314816 1074 "We model the population of the bacterial culture using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial population.\n\nWe're told that the culture initially contained $500$ bacteria, so we have ${N}_{0}=500.$\n\nTherefore, the population $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=500\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $N(3)=2\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =500\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 3} \\\\ 2\\,000 & =500\\,{e}^{3k} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{2\\,000}{500} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ k & =0.462\\,09…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈46%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314817 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314032 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314817 1069 "q-339651" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314817 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314817 1071 "The value of a savings account $A=A(t)$ after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If the account initially had $$12\\,000,$ and $A(4)=$15\\,000,$ calculate the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314817 1072 #uuid "3a50ab43-25d2-5145-8f23-9157e10a1e9c" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314817 1074 "We model the value of the savings account using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nA(t)={A}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${A}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the account initially had $$12\\,000,$ so we have ${A}_{0}=12\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the value $A$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nA(t)=12\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $A(4)=15\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(4) & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 4} \\\\ 15\\,000 & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{4k} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =\\frac{15\\,000}{12\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =1.25 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ 4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ k & =0.055\\,78…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈6%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314277 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314818 1069 "q-339658" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314818 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314818 1071 "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314818 1072 #uuid "d50aebed-a4c6-5b64-b5c3-ebb00a836540" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314818 1074 "In matrix form, the system\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nis *homogeneous* when $f≡0,$ and *inhomogeneous* when $f≢0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\n- System II is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314819 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314128 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314819 1069 "q-339661" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314819 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314819 1071 "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314819 1072 #uuid "d86591cd-cf9c-5b7c-ab46-ee83fbeda055" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314819 1074 "In matrix form, the system\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nis *homogeneous* when $f≡0,$ and *inhomogeneous* when $f≢0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since $f=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]≢0.$\n\n- System III is inhomogeneous since $f=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]≢0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314538 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314820 1069 "q-339663" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314820 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314820 1071 "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+t\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314820 1072 #uuid "1a032209-c882-5496-a788-3681657f5173" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314820 1074 "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-2, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=4, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=1, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=0, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314821 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314069 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314821 1069 "q-339664" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314821 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314821 1071 "![](images/52/528bf1bc9d6177f031b8b5504641903a11dfc2c2576e4759bf4cb7c278d0585c.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=0$ is a sink\n- $y=3$ is a source\n- $y=3$ is a node" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314821 1072 #uuid "b869e45a-6b1f-53e1-aacc-10ffe2baaefd" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314821 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=0$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=0.$ This means that $y=0$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=3$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from $y=3.$ This means that $y=3$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314822 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314332 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314822 1069 "q-339669" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314822 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314822 1071 "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)+4{e}^{t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)-4{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5){x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314822 1072 #uuid "834c59a7-4f2c-54c0-913b-f196a907cc0b" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314822 1074 "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-3, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6t) \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-2, &  & g(t)=2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since it c