i {r}^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(3)-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =6-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=\\pi -6.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [4,7].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle of radius $3,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =\\pi -6-2+6+\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{13\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi -8}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314825 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314602 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314825 1069 "q-339679" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314825 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314825 1071 "![](images/38/38270fdcb312cdce6a76e1d88bfa0117b4a69b5024b2acfd0456646471ead71f.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314825 1072 #uuid "8cd06ded-9667-5916-82c0-fdde235e2fdc" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314825 1074 "The graph consists of $4$ parts: the arc of the quarter-circle for \n$x\\in [-6,-4),$ the line segment for $x\\in (-4,-1],$ the line segment for \n$x\\in (-1,1],$ and the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in [1,5].$ \nTherefore, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-6}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the\n$x$-axis, where $x\\in [-6,-4).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle minus a quarter-circle of radius $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(2)-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-6}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=\\pi -4.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-4,-1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-1,1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3+1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,5].$ The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =bh+\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =1(4)+\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =\\pi -4+3+4+4+2\\pi  \\\\  & =7+3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314408 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314826 1069 "q-33968" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314826 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨1,3,2⟩+w⟨-5,-2,-1⟩,\\,Π:r=⟨-12,10,6⟩+s⟨1,0,4⟩+t⟨2,6,0⟩.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314826 1072 #uuid "5873c215-84fe-562e-9b4f-b99d76cb6e63" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314826 1074 "From the equation of a plane in the parametric form $r=p+tv+sw$, we have that its normal vector is $n=v\\times w.$ In our case, the normal vectors to $Π$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =⟨1,0,4⟩\\times ⟨2,6,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-24i+8j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-24,8,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane $Π$ in dot product form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩ & =⟨-12,10,6⟩\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩ & =404.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨1,3,2⟩+w⟨-5,-2,-1⟩$ into the equation of the plane in dot product form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩ & =404 \\\\ ⟨1-5w,3-2w,2-w⟩\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩ & =404 \\\\ -24(1-5w)+8(3-2w)+6(2-w) & =404 \\\\ -24+120w+24-16w+12-6w & =404 \\\\ 98w & =392 \\\\ w & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $q$ of the intersection point $Q,$ we substitute $w=4$ into the vector equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q & =⟨1-5(4),3-2(4),2-(4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-19,-5,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $Q(-19,-5,-2).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314170 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314827 1069 "q-339691" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314827 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-4x,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(2)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314827 1072 #uuid "1b7f71cd-e566-5640-b0c2-c1c9ef5ae77d" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314827 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-0=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-4{x}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-8{x}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-8{x}_{\\text{new}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-8{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ (1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-8{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y-8{x}_{\\text{new}}}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+2=2.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y-8{x}_{\\text{new}}}{1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-8\\cdot 2}{1-2\\cdot {2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{7} \\\\  & ≈2.14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈2.14.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314331 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314828 1069 "q-339692" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314828 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,(-\\frac{21\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314828 1072 #uuid "45952499-b188-5444-aa5c-78b8d9c18daf" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314828 1074 "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{21\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,(\\frac{21\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314020 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314829 1069 "q-33969