d}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈-0.67.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314580 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314830 1069 "q-339694" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314830 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(3x-2),\\,y(1)=4\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(3)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(3)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314830 1072 #uuid "219764b4-5921-5437-b598-fee3f7193725" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314830 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-1=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2) & =y \\\\ (1-2(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{(1-2(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2))} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{5-6{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+2=3.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{5-6{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5-6\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{13} \\\\  & ≈-0.31,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈-0.31.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314581 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314831 1069 "q-339695" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314831 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(2{e}^{x}+1),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314831 1072 #uuid "89aa35cd-ab7e-524a-ac84-5e49085d6090" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314831 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{y}_{\\text{new}}(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1) & =y \\\\ (1-(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{1-(2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+1)} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y}{2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y}{2{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2e} \\\\  & ≈-0.18,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈-0.18.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314207 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314832 1069 "q-339696" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314832 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(4{e}^{x}-2),\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314832 1072 #uuid "979c425c-6a86-5868-9806-54e55093eb31" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314832 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}(4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{y}_{\\text{new}}(4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}(4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-2) & =y \\\\ (1-(4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-2)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{1-(4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-2)} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{3-4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{3-4{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3-4{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3-4e} \\\\  & ≈-0.25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈-0.25.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314599 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314833 1069 "q-339698" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314833 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+2)y+{e}^{x},\\,y(1)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(2)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314833 1072 #uuid "f347e26a-3739-5da4-b5d7-58b678895d46" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314833 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =((({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+2){y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+2){y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+2){y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+2){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ (1-(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+2)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+2)} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}+{e}^{2}}{1+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}+{e}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & ≈-1.58,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-1.58.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314377 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314834 1069 "q-3397" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314834 1071 "$\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314834 1072 #uuid "84d99744-2a44-5b9d-88cd-43a5f0c291b3" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314834 1074 "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(2{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})(2{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}J \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{9}-\\frac{4}{9}I \\\\ \\frac{13}{9}I & =\\frac{2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}{13}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314266 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314835 1069 "q-339707" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314835 1071 "![](images/21/21970ba0ad8ae32ea95ad4f253d7be63aefe90316415f1aebad15c1fb3ccfd25.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=1$ is a node.\n- $y=1$ is a sink.\n- $y=-3$ is a source." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314835 1072 #uuid "fb8d059f-7809-5041-a8a8-a0657d861dc2" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314835 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/ec/eca3b223699f18fbb09c6657708b7680c4228f52323018168202351820360d9d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=1$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314121 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314836 1069 "q-339708" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314836 1071 "![](images/08/08bb9e4aed81bc739fd63e167058ff4f98ed98d022071396fbfb225271cc25b1.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=4$ is a node.\n- $y=4$ is a source.\n- $y=-1$ is a sink." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314836 1072 #uuid "2e0251a4-2b0b-541f-aa9b-c1b63489b966" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314836 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/7c/7cc5955c5268c6e933265632082a0d5be4fa87c96118b011351b71aa9ee89a7d.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=4$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314837 106