1}},\n$\\,y(0)≈$ {{field-2}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314839 1072 #uuid "85a824bf-43eb-5852-828e-ba1c3fcb5740" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314839 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(0).$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=0$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0-(-1)}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y-{x}_{\\text{new}}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(0).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y-{x}_{\\text{new}}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1-(-\\frac{1}{2})}{5} \\\\  & =0.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot y-{x}_{\\text{new}}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0.5-0}{5} \\\\  & =0.20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(-\\frac{1}{2})≈0.50,\\,y(0)≈0.20.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314326 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314467 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314840 1069 "q-339715" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314840 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2x-3y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314840 1072 #uuid "ad65dfee-7df3-53be-83ea-f3d46d2d65cc" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314840 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y-{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y-{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-1-\\frac{1}{2})}{5} \\\\  & =-0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-0.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y-{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-0.6-1)}{5} \\\\  & =-0.64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈-0.60,\\,y(1)≈-0.64.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314451 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314491 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314841 1069 "q-339717" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314841 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314841 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3x+y,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(0)$  rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(0)≈$ {{field-2}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314841 1072 #uuid "8ba7f3a1-09cc-5127-b2d5-053c21a6410a" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314841 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(0).$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=0$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0-(-1)}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-3{x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y-3{x}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(0).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y-3{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =5.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=5.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y-3{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5.5-3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(-\\frac{1}{2})≈5.50,\\,y(0)≈11.00.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314092 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314427 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314842 1069 "q-339719" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314842 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314842 1072 #uuid "f70b3c72-28f3-597e-b605-882ee6654f46" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314842 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ (1+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(2y+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)}{1+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1}{2+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1}{2+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1}{2+2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=1.3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1}{2+2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1.3+{1}^{2}+1}{2+2({1}^{2})} \\\\  & =1.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈1.30,\\,y(1)≈1.15.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314177 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314318 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314843 1069 "q-339720" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314843 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314843 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1-{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314843 1072 #uuid "41ae10ea-8c16-5d43-beba-37b186a54971" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314843 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+3({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})+\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2})+\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ (1-\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) \\\\ 2(1-\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1}{2-3({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is