oidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x-y+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.00.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314506 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314849 1069 "q-339748" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314849 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314849 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+4y,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(3),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(3)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314849 1072 #uuid "b5605bd7-5b6e-5325-91f4-de0b88b3aa11" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314849 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-1=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x+4y+(-{x}_{\\text{new}}+4{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y+4{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y+4{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y+4{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1+2=3.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1+3-5\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & ≈1.33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈1.33.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314265 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314850 1069 "q-339749" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314850 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5x+3y,\\,y(-1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(1)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314850 1072 #uuid "3355b0d7-f5f3-524b-9b8f-54391c2f5f11" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [1759