e that $y\\,(\\frac{1}{3})≈0.25.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314529 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314852 1069 "q-339756" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314852 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2}),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-1}}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314852 1072 #uuid "e7d834df-07d0-5001-b5bc-497ce36e914d" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314852 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{2}-0=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2y+({x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈1.00.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314066 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314853 1069 "q-339757" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314853 1071 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{7}{4x+12}+\\frac{5}{x+3}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314853 1072 #uuid "19499ea8-4617-5a1d-a46a-84769fc3cb44" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314853 1074 "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4x+12}+\\frac{5}{x+3} & =\\frac{7}{4(x+3)}+\\frac{5}{x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7+5(4)}{4(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4(x+3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n4(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is at $x=-3.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314630 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314854 1069 "q-339758" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314854 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314854 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-3y,\\,y(-1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\,(-\\frac{1}{3}),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(-\\frac{1}{3})≈{{field-1}}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314854 1072 #uuid "8932a695-ec28-5f7e-8f25-589d63c887b7" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314854 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{3}-(-1)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x-3y+(4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x-3y+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{4}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=-1+\\frac{2}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-1+(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & ≈-0.89.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(-\\frac{1}{3})≈-0.89.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314613 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314855 1069 "q-339763" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314855 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314855 1071 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{9}{4x-3}-\\frac{3}{8x-6}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314855 1072 #uuid "13c48095-a7eb-5b34-9bc4-abfef5bb47c3" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314855 1074 "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4x-3}-\\frac{3}{8x-6} & =\\frac{9}{4x-3}-\\frac{3}{2(4x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(2)-3}{2(4x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2(4x-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n2(4x-3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314856 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314284 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314856 1069 "q-339764" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314856 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314856 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y({x}^{2}+1),\\,y(-1)=\\frac{11}{3}\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-1}}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314856 1072 #uuid "17291882-86b0-58d5-ae80-dfa3ab9c14dc" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314856 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y({x}^{2}+1)+{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+1)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+1)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+5)+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+5) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}(3-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{4}y({x}^{2}+5) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y({x}^{2}+5)}{3-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y({x}^{2}+5)}{3-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{11}{3}((-1)^{2}+5)}{3-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{(\\frac{11}{4})} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})≈8.00.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314857 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314248 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314857 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314473 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314857 1069 "q-339769" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314857 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314857 1071 "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{1}{x+7}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} \n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314857 1072 #uuid "821dd525-f7d5-56cb-bb64-b2447eb73bf6" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314857 1074 "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{1}{x+7} & =\\frac{x+7-(x-3)}{(x-3)(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(x-3)(x+7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x+7)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-7\\,,\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=-7$ and $x=3.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314099 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314359 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314608 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314858 1069 "q-339779" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314858 1071 "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-9}-\\frac{1}{x+5}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,$\n and $\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314858 1072 #uuid "f1bf6373-5956-5320-9975-d0a5b466b7ad" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314858 1074 "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-9}-\\frac{1}{x+5} & =\\frac{x(x+5)-({x}^{2}-9)}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-{x}^{2}+9}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x+9}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x+9}{(x-3)(x+3)(x+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x+3)(x+5)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-5\\,,\\,x=-3\\,,\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=-5$,  $x=-3,$ and $x=3.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314541 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314859 1069 "q-33978" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314859 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314859 1071 "Consider the curve $r(t)=3t\\,i+2{t}^{3/2}\\,j+4\\,k,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by  $s=2(1+t)^{3/2}-2.$ Given that $R(s)$ is the parameterization of the given curve by its arc length $s,$ what is the $y$-component of $R?$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314859 1072 #uuid "0f9201e5-21fe-504c-b7c9-529d005689a4" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314859 1074 "First, since $s=2(1+t)^{3/2}-2$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =2(1+t)^{3/2}-2 \\\\ s+2 & =2(1+t)^{3/2} \\\\ \\frac{s+2}{2} & =(1+t)^{3/2} \\\\ {(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{2/3} & =1+t \\\\ t & ={(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{2/3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r({(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{2/3}-1) \\\\  & =3[{(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{2/3}-1]\\,i+2{[{(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{2/3}-1]}^{3/2}\\,j+4\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the $y$-component of $R$ is\n\n$$\n2{[{(\\frac{s}{2}+1)}^{2/3}-1]}^{3/2}.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314245 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314860 1069 "q-339791" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314860 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314860 1072 #uuid "7cfa6f7e-c7ca-5a0c-8385-839799eb336e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314860 1074 "Factoring the constant out of the integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot {e}^{x}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =5({e}^{3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =5({e}^{3}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314861 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314621 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314861 1069 "q-339797" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314861 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314861 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314861 1072 #uuid "bb1c657b-33e1-538c-a41b-0a68255fe01e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314861 1074 "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314259 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314862 1069 "q-339798" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314862 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314862 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡x}{4{x}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314862 1072 #uuid "71ed2e26-0e24-5b2f-bb68-bcacff682ddf" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314862 1074 "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡x}{4{x}^{4}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡x}{4{x}^{4}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡x}{{x}^{4}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡x}{{x}^{4}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314233 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314863 1069 "q-3398" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314863 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314863 1071 "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314863 1072 #uuid "c5988885-b8cf-5d2a-b055-96c1614a3b1c" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314863 1074 "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})(-{e}^{-x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I.$ We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =\\frac{2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314086 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314283 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314702 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314864 1069 "q-339800" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314864 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+y,\\,y(1)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Complete the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| $2$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314864 1072 #uuid "60ddfc96-62ae-580f-ba85-c7bc3da3aae5" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314864 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2}