}^{x}-\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\,{|}_{1}^{e}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\,{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =({e}^{e}-{e}^{1})-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =({e}^{e}-e)-(1-0) \\\\  & ={e}^{e}-e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314181 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314528 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314874 1069 "q-339856" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314874 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314874 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-3y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(1)$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(1)≈{{field-1}},\\,y(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314874 1072 #uuid "17cebf9d-f002-5729-b62b-0843db740e00" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314874 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x-3y)+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(1)$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0.20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=0.20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2-0.20) \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{25} \\\\  & =1.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈0.20,\\,y(2)≈1.16.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314141 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314362 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314875 1069 "q-339858" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314875 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314875 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(1)$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\n-3.11\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [1759