x}+4) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y(3\\sqrt{x}+4)}{3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y(3\\sqrt{x}+4)}{3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(3\\sqrt{0}+4)}{3\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ≈-2.67.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈-2.67.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314145 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314346 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314461 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314662 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314673 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314884 1069 "q-339876" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314884 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y=3{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314884 1072 #uuid "81480660-8b23-5d75-8411-ad962650246b" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314884 1074 "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{-x})^{′}=-{e}^{-x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(-{e}^{-x})^{′}={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n({e}^{-x})+5(-{e}^{-x})+4({e}^{-x})=3{e}^{-x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{-x})^{′}={e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})^{′}=-2{e}^{-x}+x{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(-2{e}^{-x}+x{e}^{-x})+5({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})+4(x{e}^{-x})=3{e}^{-x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-4x}+x{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314060 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314185 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314197 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314330 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314592 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314885 1069 "q-339878" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314885 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-6y=6{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{3x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{3x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314885 1072 #uuid "fda84922-b86f-5e9f-ad04-1ae70c76ef22" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314885 1074 "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{3x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{3x})^{′}=3{e}^{3x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{e}^{3x})^{′}=9{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(9{e}^{3x})+(3{e}^{3x})-6({e}^{3x})=6{e}^{3x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{3x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{3x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{3x})^{′}={e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x})^{′}=6{e}^{3x}+9x{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(6{e}^{3x}+9x{e}^{3x})+({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x})-6(x{e}^{3x})=6{e}^{3x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{3x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314074 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314151 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314256 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314534 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314578 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314886 1069 "q-339879" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314886 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-6y=-12{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314886 1072 #uuid "58612e0b-7cd3-5b46-bfa2-b039e1e27a95" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314886 1074 "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{-2x})^{′}=-2{e}^{-2x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(-2{e}^{-2x})^{′}=4{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(4{e}^{-2x})+5(-2{e}^{-2x})-6({e}^{-2x})=-12{e}^{-2x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{-2x})^{′}={e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x})^{′}=-4{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(-4{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x})+5({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x})-6(x{e}^{-2x})=-12{e}^{-2x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x}+{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314371 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314887 1069 "q-33988" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314887 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨5,3,-4⟩+t⟨-5,2,7⟩,\\,Π:r\\cdot ⟨2,3,1⟩=30.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314887 1072 #uuid "f60e171f-8d34-5a7f-bcb5-85d2254301da" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314887 1074 "Let's write the equation of the line as\n\n$$\nr=⟨5-5t,3+2t,-4+7t⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨5-5t,3+2t,-4+7t⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨2,3,1⟩ & =30 \\\\ ⟨5-5t,3+2t,-4+7t⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,1⟩ & =30 \\\\ 2(5-5t)+3(3+2t)-4+7t & =30 \\\\ 10-10t+9+6t-4+7t & =30 \\\\ 3t & =15 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$ is obtained by substituting $t=5$ into the equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨5-5(5),3+2(5),-4+7(5)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-20,13,31⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(-20,13,31).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314150 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314888 1069 "q-339885" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314888 1071 "Find the left-sided limit at $x=\\pi $ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\pi  & \\text{ for }\\,x<\\pi  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge \\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314888 1072 #uuid "0d1fa46a-bd15-5cef-a266-714f1a5d2cc1" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314888 1074 "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\pi ,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}(x+\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314325 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314889 1069 "q-339892" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314889 1071 "Find the right-sided limit at $x=8$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 8 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x}+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314889 1072 #uuid "67a261e9-7c6a-5e81-ba40-475bbacc0c16" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314889 1074 "Approaching from the right, we have $x>8,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}(\\sqrt[3]{x}+2) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8}+2 \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314492 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314697 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314890 1069 "q-339938" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314890 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3}+7 & \\text{ for }\\,x<-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-4}-1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314890 1072 #uuid "66fb8e4c-ecf8-56a9-817a-82d006285591" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314890 1074 "Approaching $x=-2$ from the left, we have $x<-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=(x+2)^{3}+7.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}[(x+2)^{3}+7] \\\\  & =(-2+2)^{3}+7 \\\\  & ={0}^{3}+7 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=4$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 4,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-4}-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x-4}-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4-4}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0}-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314167 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314527 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314891 1069 "q-339939" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314891 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x+1))-5 & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-2}+(x-2)^{2}-3 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314891 1072 #uuid "bccca18f-a63b-5416-957e-a4a5d6a04de3" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314891 1074 "Approaching $x=-1$ from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x+1))-5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x+1))-5] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (-1+1))-5 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-5 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=2$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-2}+(x-2)^{2}-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x-2}+(x-2)^{2}-3) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2-2}+(2-2)^{2}-3 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0}+{0}^{2}-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314633 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314696 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314892 1069 "q-339947" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314892 1071 "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9-{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} \n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314892 1072 #uuid "40a10555-777a-5e57-bccb-969507d27465" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314892 1074 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9-{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{(3-x)(3+x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(3-x)(3+x)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,3\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=-3$ and $x=3$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/ea/ead2334ba9281edbe8545c4d18b8e64bb72e28915d98674fe68364dfdc4e0a4e.png)" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314650 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314893 1069 "q-339949" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314893 1071 "Given that $y=\\frac{11}{4}ln⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314893 1072 #uuid "dffc415e-0ffd-5f91-84e8-ab042b2a6f3c" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314893 1074 "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{11}{4}ln⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314138 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314894 1069 "q-339950" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314894 1071 "Given that $y=-\\frac{7}{3}ln⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314894 1072 #uuid "eeedc903-d84c-5e00-bc96-dcee7bf9bc01" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314894 1074 "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{7}{3}ln⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314052 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314311 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314895 1069 "q-339951" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314895 1071 "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+5x-14}$ are\n\n$x=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314895 1072 #uuid "f2deb097-3491-5e98-9d8a-751c5a826649" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314895 1074 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+5x-14} \\\\ y & =\\frac{8}{(x-2)(x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+7)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-7,\\,x=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the vertical asymptotes are $x=-7$ and $x=2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/06/069b968e405a1302f67a085da7aff92979c95d4fca423aad3885ca6267d8cc34.png)" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314416 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314567 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314896 1069 "q-339953" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314896 1071 "From **left to right**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x-5}{{x}^{2}-4x-21}$ are\n\n$x=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314896 1072 #uuid "979e78d3-beb1-5d90-b3ff-4af79ac8856a" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314896 1074 "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x-5}{{x}^{2}-4x-21} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x-5}{(x-7)(x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-7)(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\\,x=7\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the vertical asymptotes are $x=-3$ and $x=7,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/56/56b3d93afca64c37cd48013702f2317f41935e85d5b795c34f31a76927852d44.png)" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314593 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314897 1069 "q-339960" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314897 1071 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{4x-8}{2{x}^{2}+2x-12}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314897 1072 #uuid "fa3be03f-3b98-5a24-8dee-730564cb0695" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314897 1074 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x-8}{2{x}^{2}+2x-12} & =\\frac{4(x-2)}{2(x+3)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{(x-2)}}{2(x+3)\\cancel{(x-2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+3=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-3.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314358 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314898 1069 "q-339963" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314898 1071 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{10x}{{x}^{2}+5x}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314898 1072 #uuid "6a9f93f4-b37b-56e6-8b3e-1029a061c64e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314898 1074 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10x}{{x}^{2}+5x} & =\\frac{10x}{x(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{x+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+5=0\\,⟹\\,x=-5\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-5.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314097 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314497 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314899 1069 "q-339967" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314899 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{7x+2}-1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314899 1072 #uuid "b715659f-46cf-51b3-82e4-5f218178bf7e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314899 1074 "Approaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}[3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)+2] \\\\  & =3sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0))+2 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+2 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=2$ from the right, we have $x\\ge 2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt{7x+2}-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(\\sqrt{7x+2}-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7(2)+2}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16}-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314515 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314900 1069 "q-339968" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314900 1071 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{8x-16}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314900 1072 #uuid "f0d9e3f1-f1a6-5400-9f66-35a5414cd8e5" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314900 1074 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x-16}{{x}^{2}-6x+8} & =\\frac{8(x-2)}{(x-2)(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cancel{(x-2)}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=4.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314901 1069 "q-339971" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314901 1071 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x}{{x}^{2}-4x}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314901 1072 #uuid "85d43597-e83a-569f-80a4-370b6f6da9f4" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314901 1074 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x}{{x}^{2}-4x} & =\\frac{2x(x+4)}{x(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{x}(x+4)}{\\cancel{x}(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+4)}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=4.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314413 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314902 1069 "q-339975" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314902 1071 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-16}{{x}^{2}+6x+8}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314902 1072 #uuid "b23b38f5-4680-50ab-8447-c2ad8b8f8941" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314902 1074 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-16}{{x}^{2}+6x+8} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-{4}^{2}}{(x+2)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-4)(x+4)}{(x+2)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-4)\\cancel{(x+4)}}{(x+2)\\cancel{(x+4)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-4}{x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+2=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-2.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314570 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314903 1069 "q-339976" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314903 1071 "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-8}{{x}^{2}+7x+10}$ is\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314903 1072 #uuid "f5a3d7ff-7582-5c16-b577-02baf1c5e561" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314903 1074 "We start by factoring the function as much as possible and canceling the common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-8}{{x}^{2}+7x+10} & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-4)}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{2}^{2})}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)(x+2)}{(x+5)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{(x+5)\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-2)}{x+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+5=0\\,⟹\\,x=-5\n$$\n\nSo the *only* vertical asymptote is at $x=-5.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314051 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314904 1069 "q-339994" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314904 1071 "Given the function $y=2{x}^{3}+x-3{log}_{6}⁡x,$ find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=3$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314904 1072 #uuid "b9fb0b98-97be-5fa4-a0a8-73b036fd9d03" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314904 1074 "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{xln⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}+x-3{log}_{6}⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+1-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{xln⁡6} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+1-\\frac{3}{xln⁡6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(6{x}^{2}+1-\\frac{3}{xln⁡6})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =6(3)^{2}+1-\\frac{3}{3\\cdot ln⁡6} \\\\  & =55-\\frac{1}{ln⁡6}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314152 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314905 1069 "q-340024" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314905 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314905 1071 "Given the function $y=2{x}^{3}-5{log}_{3}⁡x,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=2$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314905 1072 #uuid "1eb887e7-89db-539c-9d35-d652e9bb15c3" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314905 1074 "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-5{log}_{3}⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{3}⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{xln⁡3} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{xln⁡3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(6{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{xln⁡3})|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =6(2)^{2}-\\frac{5}{2ln⁡3} \\\\  & =24-\\frac{5}{2ln⁡3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314210 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314682 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314906 1069 "q-340025" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314906 1071 "The largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{4-3x}-\\sqrt{x+3}$ is continuous is\n\n$\\,[$\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$].$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314906 1072 #uuid "4ea70379-b7a9-5bc9-bb66-83755fd01a9f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314906 1074 "Our function consists of two radical functions, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{4-3x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-3x\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,3x\\le 4\\,⟹\\,x\\le \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{x+3}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is given by the intersection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{4}{3}]\\cap [-3,\\infty )=[\\,-3,\\frac{4}{3}]\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314437 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314907 1069 "q-340026" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314907 1071 "Given the function $y=6{log}_{2}⁡x+4{x}^{2}-3x,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314907 1072 #uuid "1b69d4c2-7ebc-59ed-aa14-566d23bb3592" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314907 1074 "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{log}_{2}⁡x+4{x}^{2}-3x) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{2}⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{xln⁡2}+4\\cdot 2x-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{xln⁡2}+8x-3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{6}{xln⁡2}+8x-3)|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{1\\cdot ln⁡2}+8(1)-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{ln⁡2}+5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314027 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314405 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314908 1069 "q-340040" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314908 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314908 1071 "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}a{x}^{2}+2x-3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 2 \\\\ 4x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=9.$ Find the values of the constants $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314908 1072 #uuid "b365b19f-c573-5f8f-9a4e-3a497f645aa0" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314908 1074 "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to be equal to $8,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $8.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x\\le 2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=a{x}^{2}+2x-3.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(a{x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & =a({2}^{2})+2(2)-3 \\\\  & =4a+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =9 \\\\ 4a+1 & =9 \\\\ 4a & =8 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=4x+b.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(4x+b) \\\\  & =4(2)+b \\\\  & =8+b\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =9 \\\\ 8+b & =9 \\\\ b & =9-8 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314446 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314521 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314641 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314909 1069 "q-340050" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314909 1071 "The largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-1}{x-1}$ is continuous is\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)⋃($\n{{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314909 1072 #uuid "3468543b-a935-5b55-9b51-1b5cc6037d5d" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314909 1074 "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-1}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)({x}^{2}+x+1)}{x-1} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $x\\ne 1.$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,1)$ and $(1,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314340 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314910 1069 "q-340056" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314910 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314910 1071 "Given that $f(x)=6{e}^{x}+ln⁡8,$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314910 1072 #uuid "6869b95a-63a5-54db-b062-b375ad3ac68d" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314910 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{x}+ln⁡8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡8) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =6{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314143 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314204 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314911 1069 "q-340062" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314911 1071 "The largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)={log}_{e}⁡(4+2x)$ is\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314911 1072 #uuid "2bcfc462-a947-5f79-9ef0-9568ba62e9fc" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314911 1074 "The logarithmic function ${log}_{e}⁡(4+2x)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n4+2x>0\\,⟹\\,2x>-4\\,⟹\\,x>-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)={log}_{e}⁡(4+2x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314192 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314912 1069 "q-340067" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314912 1071 "Given that $f(x)=9{e}^{x}+ln⁡4$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=ln⁡2$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314912 1072 #uuid "19b7181e-4102-59d9-864a-c870a873bbda" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314912 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡4$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{x}+ln⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡4) \\\\  & =9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =9{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=ln⁡2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=ln⁡2} & ={(9{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=ln⁡2} \\\\  & =9{e}^{ln⁡2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314202 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314323 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314913 1069 "q-340069" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314913 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314913 1071 "The largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)={log}_{e}⁡(10-5x)$ is\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314913 1072 #uuid "a83e9a77-8ebb-59d3-a88e-9e7019ef06bb" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314913 1074 "The logarithmic function ${log}_{e}⁡(10-5x)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n10-5x>0\\,⟹\\,5x<10\\,⟹\\,x<2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)={log}_{e}⁡(10-5x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,2).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314096 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314677 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314914 1069 "q-340082" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314914 1071 "The largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)={log}_{e}⁡(4-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314914 1072 #uuid "3ab9485a-5743-50f1-8638-78d915ca5aa4" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314914 1074 "The function consists of two parts, a logarithm and a cosine.\n\nThe cosine $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the logarithm.\n\nLikewise, the logarithmic function ${log}_{e}⁡(4-x)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n4-x>0\\,⟹\\,x<4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)={log}_{e}⁡(4-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,4).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314043 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314160 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314225 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314915 1069 "q-340088" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314915 1071 "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ The largest subset of $I$ on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is continuous is\n\n$\\,[\\frac{\\pi }{2},$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃($\n{{field-2}}\n$,$\n{{field-3}}$].$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314915 1072 #uuid "c57566f6-cede-590e-ba8d-9a9367859973" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314915 1074 "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is continuous over the entire interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}],$ but the function $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=\\pi .$ \n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ),$ and $(\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}],$ namely\n\n$$\n[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )\\cup (\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314481 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314916 1069 "q-340099" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314916 1071 "Given the function  $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{x}$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314916 1072 #uuid "ff04e1e3-e896-53d9-a3c6-32e3bf7042a0" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314916 1074 "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{2}{x}+{e}^{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1}+{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2+e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314307 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314917 1069 "q-340100" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314917 1071 "Given the function  $y=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2{e}^{x}+4$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=2$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314917 1072 #uuid "4272d4a8-55fd-5380-aaa8-0f20266b3bc5" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314917 1074 "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2{e}^{x}+4) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{x}+2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(-\\frac{3}{x}+2{e}^{x})|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}+2{e}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314556 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314918 1069 "q-340104" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314918 1071 "Given the function  $y=4ln⁡x-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{x}+5$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=2$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314918 1072 #uuid "905686ba-b3f6-58e7-998d-63ec7c2c2866" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314918 1074 "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4ln⁡x-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{x}+5) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{x}ln⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(\\frac{4}{x}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{x}ln⁡(2))|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{2}ln⁡(2) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{4}{3}ln⁡(2)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314168 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314919 1069 "q-340105" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314919 1071 "Given that $f(x)={3}^{x}+log⁡x$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314919 1072 #uuid "6065f3ff-ff9c-5643-832d-8d1ba7e4dfa6" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314919 1074 "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{xln⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x}+log⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(log⁡x) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}ln⁡(3)+\\frac{1}{xln⁡(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314387 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314920 1069 "q-340106" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314920 1071 "Given that $y=-{e}^{x}+3{log}_{5}⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314920 1072 #uuid "05a9cae0-c21b-5778-9f36-154aaf19469f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314920 1074 "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{xln⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{e}^{x}+3{log}_{5}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{5}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{xln⁡(5)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}+\\frac{3}{xln⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314316 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314921 1069 "q-340110" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314921 1071 "Given the function $y=4{e}^{x}-3{log}_{2}⁡x+7,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=3$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314921 1072 #uuid "5c352abb-1992-52cb-94e7-b48e48f5bbc8" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314921 1074 "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{x}-3{log}_{2}⁡x+7) \\\\  & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{2}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7) \\\\  & =4{e}^{x}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{xln⁡2}+0 \\\\  & =4{e}^{x}-\\frac{3}{xln⁡2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(4{e}^{x}-\\frac{3}{xln⁡2})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =4{e}^{3}-\\frac{3}{3\\cdot ln⁡2} \\\\  & =4{e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{ln⁡2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314692 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314922 1069 "q-340124" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314922 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314922 1072 #uuid "45b578e0-8138-5641-b677-427c5be958c2" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314922 1074 "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=0.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314319 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314923 1069 "q-340137" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314923 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314923 1072 #uuid "f0e13b26-bfd5-5474-9a46-4ad33e13d1cb" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314923 1074 "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta })}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314479 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314924 1069 "q-340150" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314924 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314924 1072 #uuid "024f2f9f-248f-5918-9d85-569efea4127c" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314924 1074 "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }÷4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }÷\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{({lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡0}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4} & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314243 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314925 1069 "q-340272" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314925 1071 "Given that $f(x)=7-2\\cdot {8}^{x}$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314925 1072 #uuid "ca876738-9b8d-5832-85d7-2d09df78130a" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314925 1074 "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7-2\\cdot {8}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\cdot {8}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({8}^{x}) \\\\  & =0-2(ln⁡(8){8}^{x}) \\\\  & =-2ln⁡(8){8}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314601 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314926 1069 "q-340280" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314926 1071 "Given that $f(x)={5}^{x}-6{x}^{3}+4x-1$, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314926 1072 #uuid "d2b3624c-f62d-5f81-b0de-c13d8e4bf17c" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314926 1074 "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{x}-6{x}^{3}+4x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{x})-6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{x}-6(3{x}^{2})+4(1)-0 \\\\  & =ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{x}-18{x}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{x}-18{x}^{2}+4)|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{0}-18(0)^{2}+4 \\\\  & =ln⁡(5)\\cdot 1-0+4 \\\\  & =ln⁡(5)+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314189 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314927 1069 "q-340287" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314927 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314927 1072 #uuid "1a44833b-d2d0-5994-baf0-85df4e6d1fa1" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314927 1074 "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=1$ and $v(x)=x+1,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =0, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (x+1)-1\\cdot 1}{{(x+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{(x+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{(x+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314186 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314928 1069 "q-340291" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314928 1071 "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}c\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=2 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ exists. What is the value of constant $c?$\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314928 1072 #uuid "58290058-6874-5474-bd1a-32c01d8efd66" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314928 1074 "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to exist, the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=c.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}c \\\\  & =c\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{3}-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{x}^{3}-3 \\\\  & ={(2)}^{3}-3 \\\\  & =8-3 \\\\  & =5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ c & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314040 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314929 1069 "q-340297" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314929 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{6x}{1-3x}$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314929 1072 #uuid "896d857d-c5c3-50e9-b5c4-a8009fe7f2b4" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314929 1074 "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=6x$ and $v(x)=1-3x,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =6, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot (1-3x)-6x\\cdot (-3)}{{(1-3x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-18x+18x}{{(1-3x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{(1-3x)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314417 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314930 1069 "q-340298" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314930 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314930 1072 #uuid "7883ddbf-8c25-5e40-ba73-888b49a42d52" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314930 1074 "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=x+1,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =2x, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cdot (x+1)-{x}^{2}\\cdot 1}{{(x+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2x-{x}^{2}}{{(x+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x}{(x+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314254 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314931 1069 "q-340301" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314931 1071 "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(x-1)}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ p{x}^{3}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=3.$ What is the value of the constant $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314931 1072 #uuid "d01addad-4c74-58d9-88d4-649f6209fd72" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314931 1074 "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to be equal to $3,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $3.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3{e}^{\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(x-1)} \\\\  & =3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(1-1)} \\\\  & =3{e}^{\\,\\,sin(0)} \\\\  & =3{e}^{0} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=p{x}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(p{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =p\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =p\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314488 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314932 1069 "q-340303" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314932 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314932 1072 #uuid "512fed86-0cd5-5df9-91e6-8e4e5eb5930f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314932 1074 "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =1, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-x\\cdot (2x)}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314203 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314933 1069 "q-340308" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314933 1071 "If $f(x)=\\frac{tan⁡x}{x}$,  find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314933 1072 #uuid "18258014-36f7-5eab-9f3f-292a9627b048" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314933 1074 "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=tan⁡x$ and $v(x)=x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(tan⁡x)^{′}\\cdot x-tan⁡x\\cdot (x)^{′}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot x-tan⁡x\\cdot 1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x{sec}^{2}⁡x-tan⁡x}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314300 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314934 1069 "q-340309" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314934 1071 "If $f(x)=\\frac{x}{sin⁡x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314934 1072 #uuid "10dcc430-0a77-5b8b-9487-843346dfaa0d" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314934 1074 "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=sin⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x)^{′}\\cdot sin⁡x-x\\cdot (sin⁡x)^{′}}{{(sin⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot sin⁡x-x\\cdot cos⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{sin⁡x-xcos⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314433 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314935 1069 "q-340316" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314935 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{{2}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0.$\n\nThe slope at $x=0$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314935 1072 #uuid "1df174b4-8cf7-500c-9fc1-5a7ed4adda8f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314935 1074 "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={2}^{x}$ and $v(x)={x}^{2}-1.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{({2}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-1)-{2}^{x}\\cdot {({x}^{2}-1)}^{′}}{{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}ln⁡2\\cdot ({x}^{2}-1)-{2}^{x}\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}[({x}^{2}-1)ln⁡2-2x]}{{({x}^{2}-1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\,(0) & =\\frac{{2}^{0}[({0}^{2}-1)ln⁡2-2(0)]}{{({0}^{2}-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-ln⁡2}{1} \\\\  & =-ln⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at $x=0$ is $-ln⁡2.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314165 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314936 1069 "q-340324" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314936 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}(2\\cdot {3}^{x}+x)\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314936 1072 #uuid "842ae49d-76ce-5570-92a5-8a65d571722a" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314936 1074 "Splitting up the integral into the sum of two integrals and evaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2\\cdot {3}^{x}+x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\cdot {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{1}-{3}^{0})+\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(3-1)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314703 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314937 1069 "q-340332" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314937 1071 "Given that $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314937 1072 #uuid "d40e1594-3ec0-54e3-88ea-531a4ce96db1" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314937 1074 "Let $u={x}^{2}$ and $v={x}^{2}+1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2x-{x}^{2}\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}+2x-2{x}^{3}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314279 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314938 1069 "q-340335" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314938 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1-sin⁡x}{sin⁡x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314938 1072 #uuid "23fd6f2c-8994-5050-a37a-61410033ca13" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314938 1074 "Let $u=1-sin⁡x$ and $v=sin⁡x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-cos⁡x\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{sin⁡x\\cdot (-cos⁡x)-(1-sin⁡x)\\cdot cos⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{cos⁡x(-sin⁡x-1+sin⁡x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{cos⁡x\\cdot (-1)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{sin⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{cos⁡x}{sin⁡x} \\\\  & =-csc⁡xcot⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314626 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314939 1069 "q-340337" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314939 1071 "Given that $y=\\frac{x}{cos⁡x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314939 1072 #uuid "86ab750e-4dae-544c-82a8-041d54807290" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314939 1074 "Let $u=x$ and $v=cos⁡x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-sin⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{cos⁡x\\cdot 1-x\\cdot (-sin⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot (cos⁡x+xsin⁡x\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314940 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314589 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314940 1069 "q-340341" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314940 1071 "Given that $y=\\frac{x}{ln⁡x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314940 1072 #uuid "c69eee29-5e7a-522d-bfc9-641f3d5d4893" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314940 1074 "Let $u=x$ and $v=ln⁡x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(ln⁡x\\cdot 1-x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x})}{{(ln⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{ln⁡x-1}{{(ln⁡x)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314173 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314941 1069 "q-340343" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314941 1071 "Given that $y=\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{{x}^{2}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}ln⁡3}$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314941 1072 #uuid "4ac3d430-ffd8-5d49-8cb6-f41434a00666" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314941 1074 "Let $u={log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{ln⁡x}{ln⁡3}$ and $v={x}^{2}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{xln⁡3}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{xln⁡3}-\\frac{ln⁡x}{ln⁡3}\\cdot 2x)}{({x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{x}{ln⁡3}-\\frac{2xln⁡x}{ln⁡3})}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2xln⁡x}{{x}^{4}ln⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(1-2ln⁡x)}{{x}^{4}ln⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2ln⁡x}{{x}^{3}ln⁡3} \\\\  & =(\\,1-2ln⁡x\\,)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}ln⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314113 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314942 1069 "q-340352" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314942 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314942 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+y-12?$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314942 1072 #uuid "57368a00-fbb0-5aa3-a452-73c74c695262" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314942 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+y-12 & =0 \\\\ (y+4)(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-4$ and $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-4,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/c2/c2bb2cbc11afb9bb2b25fb2105d19c2748541592c77bcecab342db69834b4fe2.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-4$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-4$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source\"." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314025 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314943 1069 "q-340354" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314943 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{3}({x}^{3}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314943 1072 #uuid "cbb60147-0687-5a1d-aa89-696b7cd382d3" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314943 1074 "Splitting up the sum and factoring out the constants, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}({x}^{3}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{-1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{3}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({3}^{4}-(-1)^{4})+2({3}^{2}-(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(81-1)+2(9-1) \\\\  & =20+16 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314337 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314944 1069 "q-340357" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314944 1071 "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a node\n\n- $y=1$ is a source\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314944 1072 #uuid "24eff55c-c123-528b-9b40-89bdd5a76ad5" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314944 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-2,$ $y=1,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=1$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=1$ (already drawn by the above property) and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-2$ points downward, the arrow just below it points downward too.\n\n![](images/b2/b2cb0682ba91ac23d418d91e7564cbb6245bf498de79b3e615f3ed2b1fcdb997.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/74/74032d1b543a7446e87b9d3c9b1737f61996cf32817a3bce4db26f229457a348.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2,$ $y=1,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=1$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314071 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314078 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314945 1069 "q-340368" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314945 1071 "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314945 1072 #uuid "f1b5b0fe-45a4-56a8-8575-f312d7f1dcc3" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314945 1074 "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi ))+\\frac{1}{18}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-9\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(0-0)+\\frac{1}{18}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314540 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314619 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314946 1069 "q-340369" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314946 1071 "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314946 1072 #uuid "77cb7a42-6cad-573a-85fb-a6a039017afb" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314946 1074 "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(1-1)+\\frac{1}{6}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314135 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314947 1069 "q-340395" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314947 1071 "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-6)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314947 1072 #uuid "d01bc09b-164a-5371-8379-a29df172f80e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314947 1074 "Splitting up the difference and factoring out the constants, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(4