nd get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4{e}^{u} \\\\  & =4{e}^{2-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=2-ln⁡(3)$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=2-ln⁡(3)} & =4{e}^{2-(2-ln⁡(3))} \\\\  & =4{e}^{ln⁡(3)} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314379 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314971 1069 "q-340503" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314971 1071 "For the function $f(x)$ and $g(x)$, you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(2)=-1,\\,{f}^{′}(2)=3,\\,g(2)=2,\\,{g}^{′}(2)=-3\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=2.$ \n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314971 1072 #uuid "d6585c8b-0768-58db-9e8c-16bcc39b4f5f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314971 1074 "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x)) & =f(x){g}^{′}(x)+g(x){f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=2$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=2} & =f(2){g}^{′}(2)+g(2){f}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314024 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314972 1069 "q-340505" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314972 1071 "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314972 1072 #uuid "c2e94a3f-da6f-5c4b-aae5-24125dac827b" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314972 1074 "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-8x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+8x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{22}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{22}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(1-0)-\\frac{1}{22}(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{55}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314676 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314973 1069 "q-34051" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314973 1071 "Find $k$, if $2{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314973 1072 #uuid "3910d8c3-7517-5b80-9efc-a8b60965d481" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314973 1074 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+k[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ k[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ k[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $k=-3.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314657 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314974 1069 "q-340510" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314974 1071 "Let $F(x)=x$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,3)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+6)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,3)$\n- $f(x+1)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314974 1072 #uuid "68a9f1f0-e085-5ed7-bf4c-69fbdf0774fa" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314974 1074 "Since $f$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,3).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+6)=f((x+3)+3)=f(x+3)=f(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true by definition of the periodic extension.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+1)=f(3)=F(3)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)=F(2)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314659 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314975 1069 "q-34052" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314975 1071 "Find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$ given that $3a-5x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -22 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314975 1072 #uuid "a30a8dd5-ba00-5881-9097-ff69e821d30a" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314975 1074 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-5x & =b \\\\ -5x & =b-3a \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}(b-3a) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}([\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -22 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3-1=-4.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314153 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314701 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314976 1069 "q-340528" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314976 1071 "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314976 1072 #uuid "0e763dfc-e659-54e6-9b52-205e19a11f13" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314976 1074 "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314444 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314977 1069 "q-340560" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314977 1071 "The rate at which the price of a particular product changes $P(t),$ after $t$ years, is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.042P.\n$$\n\nIf the price today is $$120,$ how many years will it take for the price to increase to $$750?$\nRound your answer to the nearest year." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314977 1072 #uuid "3cd19394-53ef-5fb8-bd17-0eb0faf26bf7" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314977 1074 "We model the price growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.042P.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.042t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the price today is $$120,$ so we have ${P}_{0}=120.$\n\nTherefore, the price $P$ of the product after $t$ years is\n\n$$\nP(t)=120\\,{e}^{0.042t}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find how many years it takes for the price to grow to $$750,$ we substitute $P(t)=750$ into the equation above and solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}750 & =120\\,{e}^{0.042t} \\\\ \\frac{750}{120} & ={e}^{0.042t} \\\\ {e}^{0.042t} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.042t}) & =ln\\,(\\frac{25}{4}) \\\\ 0.042t & =ln\\,(\\frac{25}{4}) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{0.042}ln\\,(\\frac{25}{4}) \\\\ t & ≈43.633\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $44$ years for the price to increase to $$750.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314402 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314978 1069 "q-34058" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314978 1071 "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314978 1072 #uuid "dffa9b37-7313-5190-ae5f-372cf9f5aaea" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314978 1074 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-1=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314077 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314979 1069 "q-340582" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314979 1071 "Find ${h}^{′}(x)$ if $h(x)=ln⁡(x)csc⁡(x).$\n\n${h}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314979 1072 #uuid "b3a61332-223a-5fc8-8ea7-10ae847de63a" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314979 1074 "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡(x)csc⁡(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}(csc⁡x)+(ln⁡(x))(-csc⁡(x)cot⁡(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{csc⁡(x)}{x}-ln⁡(x)csc⁡(x)cot⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314693 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314980 1069 "q-340589" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314980 1071 "The rate at which the population of yeast cells in a laboratory culture grows $Y(t),$ after $t$ days, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}Y}{\\text{d}t}=0.55Y.\n$$\n\nHow many days does it take for the population to triple? Round your answer to the nearest day." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314980 1072 #uuid "9a3b77e3-9271-505a-b532-ff83bec5b611" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314980 1074 "We model the population growth using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}Y}{\\text{d}t}=0.55Y.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nY(t)={Y}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.55t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Y}_{0}$ is the initial population.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the population is triple the initial number. That is,\n\n$$\nY({t}_{1})=3{Y}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y({t}_{1}) & ={Y}_{0}{e}^{0.55{t}_{1}} \\\\ 3{Y}_{0} & ={Y}_{0}{e}^{0.55{t}_{1}} \\\\ 3 & ={e}^{0.55{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.55{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =0.55{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{0.55} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈1.997\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $2$ days for the population to triple." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314640 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314981 1069 "q-340590" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314981 1071 "The rate at which the number of independent mobile applications on a digital marketplace grows $A(t),$ after $t$ years, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.35A.\n$$\n\nHow many years does it take for the number of applications to triple? Round your answer to the nearest year." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314981 1072 #uuid "2adfa444-40b8-5d0c-9139-cf04f4466b88" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314981 1074 "We model the growth in the number of applications using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.35A.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nA(t)={A}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.35t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${A}_{0}$ is the initial number of applications.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of applications is triple the initial number. That is,\n\n$$\nA({t}_{1})=3{A}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A({t}_{1}) & ={A}_{0}{e}^{0.35{t}_{1}} \\\\ 3{A}_{0} & ={A}_{0}{e}^{0.35{t}_{1}} \\\\ 3 & ={e}^{0.35{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{0.35{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =0.35{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{0.35} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈3.139\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $3$ years for the number of applications to triple." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314271 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314982 1069 "q-340595" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314982 1071 "Find ${g}^{′}(x)$ for $g(x)={e}^{x}-3cot⁡(x).$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314982 1072 #uuid "fc3f29f8-f5c9-5816-a926-df2d0b5bf36c" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314982 1074 "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,({e}^{x}-3cot⁡(x)) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}-3(-{csc}^{2}⁡(x)) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+3{csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314290 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314983 1069 "q-340597" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314983 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){csc}^{2}⁡(x)$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314983 1072 #uuid "692c831b-b188-57f9-8d8a-25236044469a" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314983 1074 "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)(-{csc}^{2}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}{x}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314618 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314984 1069 "q-340598" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314984 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=x\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)\\,-$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314984 1072 #uuid "0fe74bab-fbda-57cd-8d62-b0fe7ee7ec0e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314984 1074 "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =1\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)+x(-{csc}^{2}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)-x{csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314647 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314985 1069 "q-340599" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314985 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{ln⁡(x)}{cot⁡(x)}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $cot⁡(x)\\,+{csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\cdot ${{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | $x{cot}^{2}⁡(x)$ |" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314985 1072 #uuid "e8691b6d-0ecf-5d21-b2f3-79779cf5dbfe" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314985 1074 "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(\\frac{ln⁡(x)}{cot⁡(x)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{cot⁡(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-ln⁡(x)(-{csc}^{2}⁡(x))}{{cot}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{cot⁡(x)+xln⁡(x){csc}^{2}⁡(x)}{x})}{{cot}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{cot⁡(x)+{csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\cdot xln⁡(x)}{x{cot}^{2}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314345 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314986 1069 "q-340603" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314986 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{x}{cot⁡(x)}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $cot⁡(x)\\,+${{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | ${cot}^{2}⁡(x)$ |" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314986 1072 #uuid "e33e2c96-5cc5-507d-8909-c27b983d8b76" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314986 1074 "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x}{cot⁡(x)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(cot⁡(x))-x(-{csc}^{2}⁡(x))}{{cot}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{cot⁡(x)+x{csc}^{2}⁡(x)}{{cot}^{2}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314695 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314987 1069 "q-340608" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314987 1071 "Find the derivative of $y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)-{4}^{x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314987 1072 #uuid "da4a2c60-1cea-5b42-b245-062d281d5cf8" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314987 1074 "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)-{4}^{x}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)-{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314516 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314988 1069 "q-340611" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314988 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314988 1071 "Find the derivative of $y={e}^{x}sec⁡(x).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}sec⁡(x)($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314988 1072 #uuid "cc86df7a-ccb0-5d8a-9c1d-7584dfc21d9f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186314988 1074 "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,({e}^{x}sec⁡(x)) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}sec⁡(x)+{e}^{x}sec⁡(x)tan⁡(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}sec⁡(x)(1+tan⁡(x)