rname{cos}⁡(7x)$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315001 1072 #uuid "5b851434-5f95-5831-8b48-c6fb4f555092" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315001 1074 "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{18}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(18\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(0-0)+\\frac{1}{18}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314107 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314622 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315002 1069 "q-340681" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315002 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315002 1071 "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315002 1072 #uuid "25666762-6caa-548c-81d4-5ffe0a56d215" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315002 1074 "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-7\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(-1-(-1))-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314162 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315003 1069 "q-340690" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315003 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(1-7x)^{4}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315003 1072 #uuid "45dae178-5aad-56cb-99e8-4cae2859e574" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315003 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=1-7x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-28(1-7x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315004 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314518 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315004 1069 "q-340691" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315004 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315004 1071 "A database is being scanned by cleanup software. The rate at which the number of unprocessed records decays $R(t),$ after $t$ minutes, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-0.25R.\n$$\n\nIf there are $20\\,000$ unprocessed records at the start of the scan, how many minutes does it take for the number of unprocessed records to decrease to $1\\,000?$ Round your answer to the nearest minute." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315004 1072 #uuid "373ad6da-bb3e-5f1e-bbec-eccfa8b969de" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315004 1074 "We model the number of unprocessed records using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-0.25R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nR(t)={R}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.25t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${R}_{0}$ is the initial number of unprocessed records.\n\nWe're told that there are $20\\,000$ unprocessed records at the start of the scan, so we have ${R}_{0}=20\\,000.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of unprocessed records has decreased to $1\\,000.$ That is,\n\n$$\nR({t}_{1})=1\\,000.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,000 & =20\\,000{e}^{-0.25{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\frac{1\\,000}{20\\,000} & ={e}^{-0.25{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.05 & ={e}^{-0.25{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.05) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.25{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.05) & =-0.25{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.05)}{-0.25} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈11.982\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $12$ minutes for the number of unprocessed records to decrease to $1\\,000.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314395 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315005 1069 "q-340695" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315005 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315005 1071 "The rate at which the number of active users of a mobile app decreases $U(t),$ after $t$ weeks from launch, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}U}{\\text{d}t}=-0.04U.\n$$\n\nIf there are $40\\,000$ active users at launch, how many weeks does it take for the number of active users to decrease to $20\\,000?$  Round your answer to the nearest week." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315005 1072 #uuid "967293c6-9fbb-56f8-8f71-51865c3e9dc6" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315005 1074 "We model the number of active users using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}U}{\\text{d}t}=-0.04U.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nU(t)={U}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.04t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${U}_{0}$ is the initial number of active users.\n\nWe're told that there are $40\\,000$ active users at launch, so we have ${U}_{0}=40\\,000.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of active users has decreased to $20\\,000.$ That is,\n\n$$\nU({t}_{1})=20\\,000.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20\\,000 & =40\\,000{e}^{-0.04{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\frac{20\\,000}{40\\,000} & ={e}^{-0.04{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.5 & ={e}^{-0.04{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.5) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.04{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.5) & =-0.04{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.5)}{-0.04} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈17.329\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest integer, we conclude that it takes $17$ weeks for the number of active users to decrease to $20\\,000.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314031 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314132 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314200 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314442 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314498 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314624 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315006 1069 "q-340712" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315006 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315006 1071 "Complete the arguments in the reasoning below to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=11.$First, we apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=F(s)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nWe now examine the convergence of $F(s)$ for different values of $s:$\n\n$\\,$ For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-3}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-4}}\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $L{11}=\\frac{11}{s},$ \ndefined for\n{{field-6}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315006 1072 #uuid "6616c7a7-a7ca-51f0-bc1c-2e3ffaffd164" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315006 1074 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>0, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}$\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{11}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=11$ is $F(s)=\\frac{11}{s},$ defined for $s>0.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314076 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314347 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314422 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314483 13194139533417 