ame{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd, so $f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${b}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{even}$ and the $\\text{sine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315030 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314042 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315030 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314212 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315030 1069 "q-340798" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315030 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315030 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+5$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the {{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315030 1072 #uuid "a23b6b8d-69e5-5d60-9ec7-32ca455aba4e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315030 1074 "To check whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={sin}^{2}⁡(-x)+5 \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{2}+5 \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+5 \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ we have that $f$ is even.\n\nFor the Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is even and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd, so $f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${b}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{even}$ and the $\\text{sine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314253 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314604 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315031 1069 "q-340805" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315031 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315031 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315031 1072 #uuid "5f42137a-5f72-5f67-bdd4-7dde0b694c45" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315031 1074 "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315032 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314615 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315032 1069 "q-340837" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315032 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315032 1071 "A balloon is being deflated. The rate at which the diameter of the balloon decays $D(t),$ after $t$ seconds, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-0.20D.\n$$\n\nHow long does it take for the balloon’s diameter to reduce to $15%$ of its original diameter?  Round your answer to the nearest second." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315032 1072 #uuid "3cc8d56e-ed47-51c9-97e5-45af8b08915d" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315032 1074 "We model the diameter of the balloon using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-0.20D.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nD(t)={D}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.20t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${D}_{0}$ is the initial diameter.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the diameter of the balloon is $15%$ of its original diameter. That is,\n\n$$\nD({t}_{1})=0.15{D}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.15{D}_{0} & ={D}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.15 & ={e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.15) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.15) & =-0.20{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.15)}{0.20} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈9.486\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest second, we conclude that it takes $9$ seconds for the balloon’s diameter to be $15%$ of its original diameter." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315033 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314174 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315033 1069 "q-340839" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315033 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315033 1071 "The amount of charge stored in a battery is decreasing due to self-discharge. The rate at which the amount of stored charge decays $W(t),$ after $t$ months, can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-0.025W.\n$$\n\nHow long does it take for the amount of stored charge to be $75%$ of the original amount? Round your answer to the nearest month." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315033 1072 #uuid "d08199dc-60c1-55f4-b810-164242e2f155" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315033 1074 "We model the amount of stored charge using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-0.025W.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nW(t)={W}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.025t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${W}_{0}$ is the initial stored charge.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the amount of stored charge is $75%$ of the original amount. That is,\n\n$$\nW({t}_{1})=0.75{W}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.75{W}_{0} & ={W}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.025{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.75 & ={e}^{-0.025{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-0.025{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) & =-0.025{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)}{0.025} \\\\ {t}_{1} & ≈11.507\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest month, we conclude that it takes $12$ months for the amount of stored charge to be $75%$ of the original amount." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315034 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314176 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315034 1069 "q-340866" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315034 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315034 1071 "Let $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,5),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-5,5).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-5,0)?$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315034 1072 #uuid "a9a5c0f3-170f-537d-a310-26965c54349f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315034 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,5),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,5), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-5,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-5,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(3(-x)^{2}-2(-x)) \\\\  & =-(3{x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315035 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314699 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315035 1069 "q-340868" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315035 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315035 1071 "Let $F(x)=-4{x}^{2}-3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315035 1072 #uuid "a28b28bc-ed42-5f0b-9525-cf569542172f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315035 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-4(-x)^{2}-3(-x)) \\\\  & =-(-4{x}^{2}+3x) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315036 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314588 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315036 1069 "q-340871" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315036 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315036 1071 "Let $F(x)=-2{x}^{3}+3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315036 1072 #uuid "9e12be66-5682-57ef-8004-80dc62d4ed67" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315036 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-2(-x)^{3}+3(-x)) \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{3}-3x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}+3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315037 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314110 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315037 1069 "q-340886" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315037 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315037 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ for $s>0,$ where $f(t)=u(5-t)$ and $u$ is the unit step function.$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315037 1072 #uuid "3734f322-965c-5278-bfad-971cab7f5809" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315037 1074 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(5-t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & t\\le 5 \\\\ 0,\\, & t>5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }u(5-t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute this integral for $s>0$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}(1-{e}^{-5s})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314054 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314148 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314247 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314638 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315038 1069 "q-340899" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315038 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315038 1071 "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform \n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}.\n$$\n\n \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below. You may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t$ and $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms,\n\n$$\nL{tf(t)}={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={{field-2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}={{field-4}},\\,s>0.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315038 1072 #uuid "3bc082e0-e258-5a86-b2f6-5e93b65fda0f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315038 1074 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}f(t)}=(-1)^{n}{F}^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{tf(t)}=-{F}^{′}(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega =4,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s({s}^{2}+16)^{-1}) \\\\  & =({s}^{2}+16)^{-1}+s(-({s}^{2}+16)^{-2}\\cdot 2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+16}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}-\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}=-{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}-16}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315039 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314620 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315039 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314664 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315039 1069 "q-340922" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315039 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315039 1071 "Let $F(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315039 1072 #uuid "7f36f74d-9ded-58b3-8942-de350905b5b9" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315039 1074 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315040 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314089 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315040 1069 "q-340926" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315040 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315040 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$\n$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315040 1072 #uuid "a94eebf7-ea68-5ac3-9351-043b927df6ac" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315040 1074 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{10}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n}.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315041 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314172 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315041 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314455 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315041 1069 "q-340932" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315041 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315041 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & x\\le 4 \\\\ 1\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315041 1072 #uuid "1b3b95d4-60c8-526c-96a6-a82f623ad04d" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315041 1074 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & x\\le 4 \\\\ 1\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(-1)\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-{\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}{[{e}^{-st}]}_{0}^{4}+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}(0-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-4s}}{s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315042 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314258 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315042 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314560 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315042 1069 "q-340945" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315042 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315042 1071 "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}$ at the point $(2,2).$\n\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315042 1072 #uuid "6db0058b-56f3-5a30-bae9-0cf86c420390" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315042 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-16{x}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{16}{(2)^{3}}=-\\frac{16}{8}=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2) \\\\ 2y-4 & =x-2 \\\\ x-2\\cdot y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315043 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314255 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315043 1069 "q-340949" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315043 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315043 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{4{x}^{3}}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315043 1072 #uuid "85e8601e-9018-5cbf-862d-85832e302cc6" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315043 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=4{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=12{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (12{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}{e}^{4{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(12{x}^{2}{e}^{4{x}^{3}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =12(1)^{2}{e}^{4(1)^{3}} \\\\  & =12{e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314458 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315044 1069 "q-340969" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315044 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315044 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={5}^{x-{x}^{3}}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315044 1072 #uuid "5d403927-9339-566e-9390-9903483d0c58" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315044 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={5}^{u},\\,u=x-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={5}^{u}ln⁡(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={5}^{u}ln⁡(5)\\cdot (1-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-3{x}^{2}){5}^{x-{x}^{3}}ln⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={((1-3{x}^{2}){5}^{x-{x}^{3}}ln⁡(5))|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =(1-3(1)^{2}){5}^{1-(1)^{3}}ln⁡(5) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {5}^{0}ln⁡(5) \\\\  & =-2ln⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315045 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314178 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315045 1069 "q-341011" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315045 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315045 1071 "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=0$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $2$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})$ at $x=0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})|}_{x=0}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315045 1072 #uuid "f44134b3-2bb1-57f7-a413-41c3c3c73af6" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315045 1074 "Using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)-f(x){g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=0$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})|}_{x=0} & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(0)g(0)-f(0){g}^{′}(0)}{(g(0))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)(4)-(\\frac{1}{2})(2)}{{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-1}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [175921863150