les, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))=3{q}^{′}(x)+4{r}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=0$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))|}_{x=0} & =3{q}^{′}(0)+4{r}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =3(2)+4(-1) \\\\  & =6-4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315048 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314513 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315048 1069 "q-341029" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315048 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315048 1071 "The values of functions $q(x)$ and $r(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=1$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $q(x)$ | $r(x)$ | ${q}^{′}(x)$ | ${r}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $1$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))$ at $x=1.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))|}_{x=1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315048 1072 #uuid "7e086649-7f81-509b-a9b7-a1934dc46bb1" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315048 1074 "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))=3{q}^{′}(x)+4{r}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=1$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))|}_{x=1} & =3{q}^{′}(1)+4{r}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =3(-4)+4(1) \\\\  & =-12+4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315049 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314238 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315049 1069 "q-341031" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315049 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315049 1071 "The values of functions $q(x)$ and $r(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=-1$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $q(x)$ | $r(x)$ | ${q}^{′}(x)$ | ${r}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))$ at $x=-1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))|}_{x=-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315049 1072 #uuid "58ef6f88-a9db-5eb6-994f-124d71c3bd6c" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315049 1074 "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))=3{q}^{′}(x)+4{r}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-1$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))|}_{x=-1} & =3{q}^{′}(-1)+4{r}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2})+4(-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314021 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315050 1069 "q-341042" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315050 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315050 1071 "For the functions $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nu(4)=1,\\,{u}^{′}(4)=6,\\,v(4)=-2,\\,{v}^{′}(4)=2\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}u(x)+3v(x))$ at $x=4.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}u(x)+3v(x))|}_{x=4}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315050 1072 #uuid "89e6c8b8-1578-5971-8542-35407df630cc" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315050 1074 "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}u(x)+3v(x))=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{′}(x)+3{v}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=4$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}u(x)+3v(x))|}_{x=4} & =\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{′}(4)+3{v}^{′}(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(6)+3(2) \\\\  & =3+6 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314048 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314102 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314400 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314531 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315051 1069 "q-341050" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315051 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315051 1071 "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform \n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7t}.\n$$\n\n \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below. You may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>\\omega .\n$$\n\nLet $f(t)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7t$ and $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms,\n\n$$\nL{tf(t)}={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={{field-2}},\\,s>7.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7t}={{field-4}},\\,s>7.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315051 1072 #uuid "ed378e80-6795-5ad0-8973-5105f9a33be1" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315051 1074 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}f(t)}=(-1)^{n}{F}^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{tf(t)}=-{F}^{′}(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega =7,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-49},\\,s>7.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-49}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s({s}^{2}-49)^{-1}) \\\\  & =({s}^{2}-49)^{-1}+s(-({s}^{2}-49)^{-2}\\cdot 2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-49}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}}-\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-49-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}+49}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7t}=-{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+49}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}},\\,s>7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315052 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314612 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315052 1069 "q-341053" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315052 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315052 1071 "The height $h$ (in feet) of a small rocket $t$ seconds after it is launched can be modeled by the function $h(t)=-16{t}^{2}+128t.$ Find the instantaneous rate at which the rocket's height is changing with respect to time at the moment when its height is equal to $256$ feet.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change of the rocket's height when its height is $256$ feet is\n{{field-1}} feet per second." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315052 1072 #uuid "d89227d7-9d52-5005-a858-9abda23f0d9e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315052 1074 "Since we are given that the rocket's height is $256$ feet, the time can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(t) & =256 \\\\ -16{t}^{2}+128t & =256 \\\\ -16{t}^{2}+128t-256 & =0 \\\\ -16({t}^{2}-8t+16) & =0 \\\\ -16(t-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (t-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t-4 & =0 \\\\ t & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous rate at which the rocket's height is changing when $t=4$ seconds is equal to ${h}^{′}(4).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $h(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-16{t}^{2}+128t) \\\\  & =-32t+128,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=4$ gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(4)=-32(4)+128=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the rocket's height is changing when $t=4$ seconds is $0$ feet per second." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315053 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314536 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315053 1069 "q-341062" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315053 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315053 1071 "![](images/34/34966965452968c2f186efcfbec70591eded02e926eaaac8b90409381b2c8da8.png)\n\nThe area of an equilateral triangle is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the area of the equilateral triangle is increasing with respect to its side length at the moment when its side length is equal to $s=4\\,\\text{in}.$  \n\n*Hint*: The area of an equilateral triangle is $A=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}{s}^{2}.$ \n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when its side length is $4$ inches is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315053 1072 #uuid "d9d4d87e-59e9-54b8-822f-cb78eea065ce" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315053 1074 "We know that the area of the equilateral triangle with side $s$ is given by  \n\n$$\nA=A(s)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $A(s)$  with respect to $s$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}{s}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\cdot (2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}s,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $s=4$ gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(4)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 4=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at the moment when the length of the side is $s=4\\,\\text{in}$ is $2\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315054 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314503 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315054 1069 "q-341104" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315054 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315054 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y=2x+arccotx$ at $x=0.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315054 1072 #uuid "3d8da40e-cfdf-5c60-a36a-cdb26f7bb8fa" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315054 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $arccot$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccot\\,x)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+arccotx) \\\\  & =2+(-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={y}^{′}(0) & =2-\\frac{1}{1+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315055 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314575 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315055 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314631 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315055 1069 "q-341107" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315055 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315055 1071 "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315055 1072 #uuid "f2c45534-de22-5957-94a0-9fb0be556b3e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315055 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)=\\frac{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{2t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2 \\\\ L{{e}^{-2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+2},\\,s>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{2t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{s+2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+2)+(s-2)}{(s-2)(s+2)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (2,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-2,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(2,\\infty )\\cap (-2,\\infty )=(2,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>2.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314447 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314610 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315056 1069 "q-341109" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315056 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315056 1071 "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315056 1072 #uuid "25fdc2a1-548f-5db7-a267-7ea03400402a" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315056 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}+{e}^{-4t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4t}+{e}^{-4t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-4t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-4t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4 \\\\ L{{e}^{-4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+4},\\,s>-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{s+4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+4)+(s-4)}{(s-4)(s+4)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{4t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (4,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-4t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-4,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(4,\\infty )\\cap (-4,\\infty )=(4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>4.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314199 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315057 1069 "q-341115" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315057 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315057 1071 "Calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $f(x)=arcsec({e}^{x})$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡10.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315057 1072 #uuid "7de810d1-79ef-5695-be73-3f0627f342f6" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315057 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $arcsec$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arcsec\\,x)=\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[arcsec({e}^{x})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|{e}^{x}|\\sqrt{{({e}^{x})}^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}-1}}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve at $x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡10$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡10) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)/2)}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314035 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315058 1069 "q-341121" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315058 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315058 1071 "Calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $f(x)=arccsc(\\sqrt{x})$ at $x=2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315058 1072 #uuid "f221de46-11bc-5772-aa56-5d0d13b544a3" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315058 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $arccsc$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccsc\\,x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[arccsc(\\sqrt{x})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{|\\sqrt{x}|\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}\\sqrt{x-1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2-1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314115 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314639 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315059 1069 "q-341127" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315059 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315059 1071 "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315059 1072 #uuid "e8a3cfa3-5dd8-5a89-8d0e-84651dbd2d63" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315059 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that the sine function can be defined in terms of complex exponentials as follows: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)=\\frac{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}-{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}-{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}-\\frac{{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}}-L{\\frac{{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}}-\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}}=\\frac{1}{s-\\text{i}\\omega },\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-12\\text{i}},\\,s