field-2}},\\,s>4.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4t}={{field-4}},\\,s>4.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315071 1072 #uuid "7344131a-f847-5800-9b21-f09ea79674c8" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315071 1074 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}f(t)}=(-1)^{n}{F}^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={F}^{″}(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega =4,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16},\\,s>4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s({s}^{2}-16)^{-1}) \\\\  & =({s}^{2}-16)^{-1}+s(-({s}^{2}-16)^{-2}\\cdot 2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-16}{({s}^{2}-16)^{2}}-\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}-16)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}+16}{({s}^{2}-16)^{2}} \\\\ {F}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{{s}^{2}+16}{({s}^{2}-16)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{{s}^{2}+16}{({s}^{2}-16)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{({s}^{2}+16)^{′}\\cdot ({s}^{2}-16)^{2}-({s}^{2}+16)\\cdot {(({s}^{2}-16)^{2})}^{′}}{({s}^{2}-16)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{(2s)({s}^{2}-16)^{2}-({s}^{2}+16)\\cdot 2({s}^{2}-16)\\cdot 2s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2s({s}^{2}-16)^{2}-4s({s}^{2}+16)({s}^{2}-16)}{({s}^{2}-16)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2s({s}^{2}-16)-4s({s}^{2}+16)}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2{s}^{3}-32s-4{s}^{3}-64s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{s}^{3}+96s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the qu