}{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩} \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314120 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314367 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315089 1069 "q-341336" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315089 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315089 1071 "Let $F(x)=-3x+2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315089 1072 #uuid "ac9732d3-1086-5e0e-8f66-b1641de82b66" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315089 1074 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-3x+2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-3x+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-3x+2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-3 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[(-3x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{3}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-3\\pi +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{3}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-3\\pi +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-2+\\frac{3}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-3\\pi +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-3\\pi +2)(-1)^{n}-2)=\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi -2)+2).\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi -2)+2)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314208 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314432 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315090 1069 "q-341340" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315090 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315090 1071 "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315090 1072 #uuid "22b987d7-9005-5317-ab10-48027150c40e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315090 1074 "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(1-1)+\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314241 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314683 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315091 1069 "q-341375" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315091 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315091 1071 "![](images/f2/f223c6b96b4ce7e522d1529145ae90d7d68927e3234aa891a7be30a345355a9e.png)\n\nFrom **left to right**, the function $y=f(x)$ above is left-continuous but is not continuous at\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}} $\\,$ and  \n$\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315091 1072 #uuid "8bd7b2d4-3e56-5a68-9aee-fc5e482550c4" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315091 1074 "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function is not left-continuous because it's undefined at this point. $\\,\\times $\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=2=f(1).\\,✓\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=0=f(3).\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, from **left to right**, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous but is right-continuous at $x=1$ and $x=3.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314147 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314590 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315092 1069 "q-341379" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315092 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315092 1071 "![](images/f4/f4f20d86a96988d657aeac166b6228cc938523e0ba6100908725aa83b6c88781.png)\n\nFrom **left to right**, the function $y=f(x)$ above is not continuous but is right-continuous at\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}} $\\,$ and  \n$\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315092 1072 #uuid "ad67db9d-a2b0-58de-bf3b-8e10dfede910" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315092 1074 "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-3$, $x=-1,$ and $x=1.$\n\n- At $x=-3$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}f(x)=-2=f(-3).\\,✓\n$$\n\n- At $x=-1$ the function is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}f(x)=0\\ne -2=f(-1).\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=0=f(1).\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, from **left to right**, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous but is right-continuous at\n$x=-3$ and $x=1.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315093 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314401 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315093 1069 "q-341381" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315093 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315093 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-2x+3$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315093 1072 #uuid "44334d32-3a61-5824-a7c7-cbdf4232a9e4" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315093 1074 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-2x+3$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-2x+3\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[(-2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{2}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-2\\pi +3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{2}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-2\\pi +3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-3+\\frac{2}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-2\\pi +3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-2\\pi +3)(-1)^{n}-3)=\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(2\\pi -3)+3).\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17