\\\  & =\\frac{2s+48}{{s}^{2}-2s-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>4.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314105 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314350 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314524 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315096 1069 "q-341390" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315096 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)=u(t-3)$ and $u$ is the unit step function.\n\n$F(s)=L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}},\nvalid for $s\\,$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315096 1072 #uuid "9db19fd8-5680-5d48-9d86-5812bc3bb368" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315096 1074 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<3 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }u(t-3){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{s}(0-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s},\\,s>0.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314317 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315097 1069 "q-341395" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315097 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $L{{f}^{″}(t)}$ for $s>1,$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ ${f}^{′}(0)=-1,$ $f(0)=5,$ and\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{5s+4}{(s-1)(s+2)},\\,s>1.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${f}^{′}$ is continuous and ${f}^{″}$ piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315097 1072 #uuid "4cf95188-41d8-51e5-a4ad-908bfec8099f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315097 1074 "Suppose $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ where $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ If ${f}^{′}(t)$ is continuous and ${f}^{″}(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ then\n\n$$\nL{{f}^{″}(t)}={s}^{2}L{f(t)}-sf(0)-{f}^{′}(0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of ${f}^{″}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{″}(t)} & ={s}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5s+4}{(s-1)(s+2)}-s\\cdot 5-(-1) \\\\  & ={s}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5s+4}{(s-1)(s+2)}-(5s-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}(5s+4)-(5s-1)(s-1)(s+2)}{(s-1)(s+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{s}^{3}+4{s}^{2}-(5{s}^{3}+4{s}^{2}-11s+2)}{(s-1)(s+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{11s-2}{(s-1)(s+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>1.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314228 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315098 1069 "q-341398" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315098 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ for $s>0,$ where $f(t)=u(8-t)$ and $u$ is the unit step function.$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315098 1072 #uuid "bb684e6a-ec18-594d-bf56-9e167c4180ca" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315098 1074 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(8-t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & t\\le 8 \\\\ 0,\\, & t>8\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }u(8-t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute this integral for $s>0$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{0}^{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-8s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}(1-{e}^{-8s})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314349 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315099 1069 "q-341406" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315099 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315099 1071 "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,10),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-10,10).$ What is the value of $f(-6)?$\n\n$f(-6)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315099 1072 #uuid "8568629c-0cf4-59ec-a9e0-385305895f8d" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315099 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,10),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,10), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-10,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-10,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}-2(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-6) & =-(-6)^{2}-2(-6)=-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314343 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315100 1069 "q-34142" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315100 1071 "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315100 1072 #uuid "5d50385e-e4f4-5265-bb61-4639aa5b495f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315100 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]=-3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]=-3{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-3{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314574 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315101 1069 "q-341431" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315101 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315101 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [-1,2].$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5,\\, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ -2,\\, & x=1,\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3),\\,h(x)={e}^{1-x}.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315101 1072 #uuid "345c0689-c82d-5948-9d0a-e54fa56f1266" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315101 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,2]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,2]$ except at the point $x=1,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=6\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,2]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,2].$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-1,2]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-1,2].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,g,$ and $h.$\"" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314509 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315102 1069 "q-341432" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315102 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315102 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=-\\frac{2}{x+1},\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ -1,\\, & x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315102 1072 #uuid "cbe76963-8081-5e98-a845-55fadb362b17" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315102 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ except at the point $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=0\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,g,$ and $h.$\"" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314361 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315103 1069 "q-341433" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315103 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-1,\\, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 2,\\, & x=1,\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,h(x)={2}^{x+1}.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315103 1072 #uuid "b4ac75fc-3ab7-5f60-a1ab-08172f4bfbf5" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315103 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ except at the point $x=1,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=0\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314700 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315104 1069 "q-341438" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315104 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,2].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1},\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x+1,\\, & x<1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1,\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315104 1072 #uuid "4e284472-56cd-598f-90d5-d79bbb6a47c5" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315104 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=1$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,2].$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,2]$ except at the point $x=1,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x)=3,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314292 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315105 1069 "q-341450" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315105 1071 "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=2$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n\nIf $h(x)=(-2f(x)-3)(g(x)+1),$ find the value of ${h}^{′}(2).$\n\n${h}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315105 1072 #uuid "dbce6b68-bc7d-5561-affe-33130a0cdf09" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315105 1074 "Explanation:\n\nThe product rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(uv)=u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-2f(x)-3$ and $v=g(x)+1.$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =-2{f}^{′}(x)(g(x)+1)+(-2f(x)-3){g}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=2$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =-2{f}^{′}(2)(g(2)+1)+(-2f(2)-3){g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-2(2)(-3+1)+(-2(4)-3)(5) \\\\  & =-4(-2)+(-8-3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =8+(-11)(5) \\\\  & =8-55 \\\\  & =-47.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314373 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314625 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315106 1069 "q-341451" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315106 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}3,\\, & t\\le 5 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\, & t>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315106 1072 #uuid "dd8bfbcf-088b-5c81-bfda-8fc9e642ff98" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315106 1074 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}3,\\, & t\\le 5 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\, & t>5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{3t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{3t-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 3.$\n\nFor $s>3,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}3{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{s}{[{e}^{-st}]}_{0}^{5}+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{e}^{(3-s)t}}{3-s})|}_{5}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{3-s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{(3-s)a}-{e}^{5(3-s)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{3-s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{5(3-s)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{5(3-s)}}{s-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>3.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314205 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314645 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315107 1069 "q-341464" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315107 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t},\\, & t\\le 2 \\\\ 2\\, & t>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315107 1072 #uuid "0dfe2c1a-430d-5eed-bc5d-17ce89a52f56" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315107 1074 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t},\\, & t\\le 2 \\\\ 2\\, & t>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{3t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{(3-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-s}{[{e}^{(3-s)t}]}_{0}^{2}+{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{-2{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-2s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-2s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2(3-s)}-1}{3-s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314333 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314474 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315108 1069 "q-341465" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315108 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315108 1071 "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t},\\, & t\\le 1 \\\\ 1\\, & t>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315108 1072 #uuid "2ac112a6-5f62-58cf-9bd0-a4369a549356" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315108 1074 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t},\\, & t\\le 1 \\\\ 1\\, & t>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{5t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\