e piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t},\\, & t\\le 4 \\\\ 2\\, & t>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{(1-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-s}{[{e}^{(1-s)t}]}_{0}^{4}+{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{-2{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-4s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4(1-s)}-1}{1-s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-4s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314562 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315110 1069 "q-341468" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315110 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315110 1071 "The values of functions $h(t)$ and $z(t)$ and their derivatives at $t=4$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $h(t)$ | $z(t)$ | ${h}^{′}(t)$ | ${z}^{′}(t)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n\nIf $f(t)=\\frac{2h(t)-5}{1-z(t)},$ compute ${f}^{′}(4).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [175921