text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-3t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-(3+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-(3+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-(3+s)}{[{e}^{-(3+s)t}]}_{0}^{1}+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}4{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{-4{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}-\\frac{4}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}-\\frac{4}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-(3+s)}}{3+s}+\\frac{4{e}^{-s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314480 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315112 1069 "q-341492" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315112 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(1.5,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X<2)=0.5910,$ use the table value to find ${\\sigma }^{2},$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ |\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315112 1072 #uuid "e50cad91-21d9-539e-b085-381c16d9469f" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315112 1074 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2)=0.5910\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le 2)=0.5910\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =0.5910 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{2-1.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5910 \\\\ Φ\\,(\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5910 \\\\ \\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5910)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.23)=0.5910\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.5910)=0.23.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & =0.23 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.5}{0.23} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈2.173\\,91,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}=(2.173\\,91)^{2}≈4.73.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314428 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315113 1069 "q-341495" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315113 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=4\\sqrt{x}+3tan⁡x.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315113 1072 #uuid "d99ea33e-6fab-5224-901c-7ede7395140b" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315113 1074 "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\sqrt{x}+3tan⁡x) \\\\  & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})+3({sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+3{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314070 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315114 1069 "q-341576" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315114 1071 "![](images/ef/ef59e4b5c39770c5bcade3475a23d2631d2db10fea4fd7c925be092bbf819e61.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is not continuous but is right-continuous at\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315114 1072 #uuid "9e5a6aa8-3cb9-561d-a3e8-9decbbe58645" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315114 1074 "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-1$ and $x=3.$\n\n- At $x=-1$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}f(x)=1=f(-1).\\,✓\n$$\n\n- At $x=3$ the function is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(3).\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous but is right-continuous at $x=-1.$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315115 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314449 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315115 1069 "q-341608" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315115 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315115 1071 "Given that $x=-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+5{y}^{2},$ express $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $y.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | ${y}^{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315115 1072 #uuid "aed30416-75f3-525a-aed4-35612b1daf20" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315115 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+5{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-2{y}^{-1}+5{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2})+5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2(-2{y}^{-3})+5(2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}+10y \\\\  & =\\frac{4+10{y}^{4}}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4+10{y}^{4}}.\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314242 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315116 1069 "q-341628" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315116 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=u(x-2)+u(x-6),\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\,h(x)=⌊\\frac{x}{2}⌋\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌊x⌋$ is the floor function." 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315116 1072 #uuid "231c2028-b69c-5efc-bb77-83f962d2656e" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true] [17592186315116 1074 "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=6$ and $x=2,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=…,-4,-2,0,2,4,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/b9/b9ffe76f612fdc577d64526ebc3322b81a87ec3856040c1c8edccb1be3b6fe5d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139533417 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0016720f-74fa-55b6-bf01-800b13e638e0" 17592186311965 "answer-001fcbcc-60ad-54b8-bf93-97fb40559ab2" 17592186311966 "answer-002bef8f-5454-5d6b-b487-b895705fe836" 17592186311967 "answer-002f802b-634d-5124-a447-a5710d29370e" 17592186311968 "answer-003ae2a8-0c61-5d93-977d-2477a6a8b0fc" 17592186311969 "answer-00485c3e-bd14-5f3f-9feb-953a2cffcf4d" 17592186311970 "answer-007ef37a-9f8a-5f38-8f68-3dfa9842b42f" 17592186311971 "answer-00e95e33-347b-57a5-99b1-b6350c6a1b82" 17592186311972 "answer-00fa78b3-f990-59e4-9c5d-d0332b6aff1c" 17592186311973 "answer-012fcbcb-91f5-5d84-baae-8e40c8a9ff5e" 17592186311974 "answer-01360f92-7aa0-57d4-94ae-48f679e6d3c8" 17592186311975 "answer-013952cf-7a2f-5afb-ad2d-57b4ac1a72df" 17592186311976 "answer-01a76f83-aec1-51bf-9ad9-5187b63b547d" 17592186311977 "answer-01de35f4-43d4-5ff4-8c4b-172a9724eed9" 17592186311978 "answer-01ef3a8b-c9f4-5529-bdcb-9407e9493ae6" 17592186311979 "answer-01f1f01f-edf5-583a-939f-94b3051cfa20" 17592186311980 "answer-0204b555-28bf-5807-b7e8-f6537e0c3f73" 17592186311981 "answer-0267a896-74c5-5b6a-b27b-8e244334504d" 17592186311982 "answer-027eecbf-7d9e-56b4-a605-18bbb53e14b6" 17592186311983 "answer-02879327-9074-58b7-a673-9af647dd76a0" 17592186311984 "answer-02b63af9-4f30-589d-b300-e21c0d2d64e9" 17592186311985 "answer-02bc3189-1d45-5e38-b2ab-7948fb50c3ca" 17592186311986 "answer-02bee4f3-b655-5e19-8244-51de7d7873c8" 17592186311987 "answer-02dad420-9792-5a51-a42c-f70a7aa18009" 17592186311988 "answer-02fd4ccf-3290-5aa8-a9db-49b67da2b464" 17592186311989 "answer-030817a4-5c86-5a0e-952e-cf5f2fb1ae18" 17592186311990 "answer-030b203c-bf99-57ae-aa05-b07007ad6946" 17592186311991 "answer-033fde71-9d4a-5cae-8772-19e43b24a192" 17592186311992 